\section{Singular Valued Decomposition (SVD)}

In following theorems and remarks maybe repeated to help illuminate various
structures and relationships.

\begin{definition}
The vector space of $\mathbb{C}$-linear functionals $f$%
\begin{equation}
f:X\rightarrow\mathbb{C}%
\end{equation}
forms a vector space $X^{d}$ that is called the \emph{dual space} of $X.$
\end{definition}

\begin{notation}
The pairing of a functional $f$ in the dual space $X^{d}$ and an element $x\in
X$ is denoted by a bracket
\begin{equation}
f\left(  x\right)  =\left\langle f,x\right\rangle .
\end{equation}
This pairing defines a nondegenerate $\mathbb{C}$-bilinear mapping%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle ,\right\rangle :X\times X\rightarrow\mathbb{C}%
\end{equation}
called the natural pairing.
\end{notation}

\begin{remark}
Various topologies on $X$ can be considered leading to different versions of
continuity of elements of $X^{d},$ the algebraic dual of $X$ contains all
topological duals and contains linear functional that are not necessarily
bounded nor continuous.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
If $X$ is a normed space with norm $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{X}$ then
$X^{d}$ is the space of continuous (wrt $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{X})$
linear functionals defined on $X.$
\end{remark}

Let $\mathcal{V}$ and $\mathcal{W}$ be \emph{normed} spaces with norms
$\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{V}}$ and $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{w}$
respectively then the linear map $M$ from $\mathcal{V}$ into $\mathcal{W}$
\begin{equation}
M:\mathcal{V}\rightarrow\mathcal{W},\quad M\in\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}\right)
\end{equation}
has an associated dual map
\begin{equation}
M^{d}:\mathcal{W}^{d}\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{d},\quad M^{d}\in\mathcal{L}%
\left(  \mathcal{W}^{d},\mathcal{V}^{d}\right)
\end{equation}
defined by%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle w^{d},Mv\right\rangle =\left\langle M^{d}w^{d},v\right\rangle .
\end{equation}
The map $M$ is a surjection (\emph{submersion}) if
\begin{equation}
\operatorname*{Range}\left\{  M\right\}  =\mathcal{W}%
\end{equation}
if it is injective i.e.
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{  M\right\}  =\phi
\end{equation}
it is an \emph{immersion} and it \emph{embeds} $\mathcal{V}$ in $\mathcal{W}.$

If $M$ is bounded then
\begin{equation}
\operatorname*{Dom}\left\{  M\right\}  =\mathcal{V}%
\end{equation}
in general
\begin{equation}
\operatorname*{Dom}\left\{  M\right\}  =\left\{  \left.  \overset{}{v\in
V}\right\vert \left\Vert Mv\right\Vert _{\mathcal{W}}<\infty\right\}  .
\end{equation}


\begin{remark}
$\mathcal{V}$ is naturally embedded in $\mathcal{V}^{dd}$
\begin{equation}
v\rightarrow v^{dd}%
\end{equation}
by%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle v^{dd},f\right\rangle =\left\langle f,v\right\rangle ,\,\forall
v\in\mathcal{V}\text{ and }\forall f\in\mathcal{V}^{d}.
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Every vector space has bases, that is, maximal linearly independent subsets.
Every vector in a vector space can be written in a unique way as a finite
linear combination of the elements in these bases. A basis for an infinite
dimensional vector space is also called a \emph{Hamel} basis. The proof that
every vector space has a basis uses the axiom of choice. There is no practical
way to find a Hamel basis in general, which means we have little use for the
concept of a basis for a general infinite (especially uncountable) dimensional
vector space.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
A \emph{Schauder} basis or countable basis is similar to the usual (Hamel)
basis of a vector space; the difference is that Hamel bases use linear
combinations that are finite sums, while for Schauder bases they may be
infinite sums. This makes Schauder bases more suitable for the analysis of
infinite-dimensional topological vector spaces including Banach spaces. Let
$V$ denote a topological vector space over the field $\mathbb{F}$. A Schauder
basis is a sequence $\{e_{n}\}$ of elements of $V$ such that for every element
$v\in V$ there exists a unique sequence $\{\left.  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
v\right)  _{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq\infty\}$ of scalars in $\mathbb{F}$ so
that%
\begin{equation}
v=\sum_{0\leq j\leq\infty}e_{j}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}%
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The convergence of the infinite sum is implicitly in that of the ambient
topology, i.e.,%
\begin{equation}
\lim_{j\rightarrow\infty}\left\{  \sum_{0\leq j\leq\infty}e_{j}\mathcal{R}%
_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}\right\}  =v
\end{equation}
but can be reduced to only weak convergence in a normed vector space (such as
a Banach space). Unlike a Hamel basis, the elements of the basis must be
ordered since the series may not converge unconditionally.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Note that some authors define Schauder bases to be countable (as above), while
others use the term to include uncountable bases. In either case, the sums
themselves always are countable. An \emph{uncountable Schauder} basis is a
\emph{linearly ordered set} rather than a sequence, and each sum inherits the
order of its terms from this linear ordering. They can and do arise in
practice. As an example, a separable Hilbert space can only have a countable
Schauder basis but a non-separable Hilbert space may have an uncountable one.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Let $X$ be Banach space. A biorthogonal system $\{e_{x}^{b};e_{x}%
\},x\in{\Large \U{3b1} }$ in $X$ is a Markusevich basis if
\begin{equation}
\overline{\operatorname*{linspan}_{x\in{\Large \U{3b1} }}\left\{
e_{x}\right\}  }=X
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\left\{  \left.  e_{x}^{b}\right\vert x\in{\Large \U{3b1} }\right\}
\end{equation}
seperates the points of $X.$
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
In functional analysis, a \emph{Markushevich} basis is a biorthogonal system
that is both \emph{complete} and \emph{total}.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
In a separable space, biorthogonality is not a substantial obstruction to a
Markuschevich basis; any spanning set and separating functionals can be made
biorthogonal. But it is an open problem whether every separable Banach space
admits a Markushevich basis with%
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert e_{x}\right\Vert =\left\Vert e_{x}^{b}\right\Vert =1\quad\forall
x\in{\Large \U{3b1} }.
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Every Schauder basis of a Banach space is also a Markuschevich basis, but the
converse is not true in general.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}
of the \emph{injection} $\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}e}:$

\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathcal{V}$ can be embedded in $\mathcal{V}^{d}$ i.e.
$\mathcal{V\hookrightarrow V}^{d}$ by using the dual bases $\left(
\mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  $ obtained from $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $
\begin{equation}
\left\langle e_{j}^{d},e_{k}\right\rangle =\delta_{jk},\,1\leq j,k\leq r
\end{equation}
by the injection
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}e}:\mathcal{V\hookrightarrow V}^{d},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
e_{j}^{d}=\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}e}\left(  e_{j}\right)
\end{equation}
giving
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}e}\left(  v\right)  =\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}e}\left(
\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}e_{j}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}\right)
=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}e_{j}^{d}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}%
=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}e_{j}^{d}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v^{d}\right)  _{j}=v^{d}.
\end{equation}


\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}e}\in\mathcal{B}_{00}\left(  V^{d},V\right)
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\left\langle v^{d},\mu\right\rangle =\left\langle \mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}e}\left(
v\right)  ,\mu\right\rangle =\left\langle \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}e_{j}%
^{d}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j},\mu\right\rangle =\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}\left\langle e_{j}^{d},\mu\right\rangle \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)
_{j}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}e_{j}^{d}\left(  \mu\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
v\right)  _{j},\,\forall\mu\in\mathcal{V}.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}

\item $\mathcal{V}$ can be embedded in $\mathcal{V}^{d}$ i.e.
$\mathcal{V\hookrightarrow V}^{d}$ by using the $\left\langle \left.
{}\right\vert \right\rangle $-biorthogonal dual bases $\left(  \mathbf{e}%
\right)  $ and $\left\vert \mathbf{e}^{b}\right\rangle $
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left.  e_{j}^{b}\right\vert e_{k}\right\rangle =\delta
_{jk},\,1\leq j,k\leq r
\end{equation}
defined by
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\sigma_{e}\left(  e\right)  }=\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}e}\left(
\sigma_{e}\right)  =\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}e}\circ\sigma_{e}%
:\mathcal{V\hookrightarrow V}^{d},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
e_{j}^{d}=\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\sigma_{e}\left(  e\right)  }\left(
e_{j}\right)
\end{equation}
giving
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\sigma_{e}\left(  e\right)  }\left(  v\right)
=\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\sigma_{e}\left(  e\right)  }\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}e_{j}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}e_{j}^{d}\overline{\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}e_{j}^{d}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v^{d}\right)  _{j}=v^{d}.
\end{equation}


\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\sigma_{e}\left(  e\right)  }\in\mathcal{B}_{anti00}\left(
V^{d},V\right)
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\left\langle v^{d},\mu\right\rangle =\left\langle \mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}%
\sigma_{e}\left(  e\right)  }\left(  v\right)  ,\mu\right\rangle =\left\langle
\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}e_{j}^{d}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}%
,\mu\right\rangle =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left\langle e_{j}^{d},\mu\right\rangle
\overline{\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}e_{j}%
^{d}\left(  \mu\right)  \overline{\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}%
},\,\forall\mu\in\mathcal{V}.
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\left\vert e\right\rangle }\left(  v\right)  =\sum_{1\leq
j\leq r}\left\langle e_{j}^{b}\right\vert \overline{\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
v^{d}\right)  _{j}}.
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\left\langle v^{d},\mu\right\rangle =\left\langle \left.  v^{d}\right\vert
\mu\right\rangle =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\overline{\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
v\right)  _{j}}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \mu\right)  _{j}.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}

\item $\mathcal{V}$ can be embedded in $\mathcal{V}^{d}$ i.e.
$\mathcal{V\hookrightarrow V}^{d}$ by using the $\left(  \left.  {}\right\vert
\right)  $-biorthogonal dual bases $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ and
$\left\vert \mathbf{e}^{b}\right)  $
\begin{equation}
\left(  \left.  e_{j}^{b}\right\vert e_{k}\right)  =\delta_{jk},\,1\leq
j,k\leq r
\end{equation}
defined by
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\left\vert e\right)  }:\mathcal{V\hookrightarrow V}^{d},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
e_{j}^{d}=\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\left\vert e\right)  }\left(  e_{j}\right)
\end{equation}
giving
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\left\vert e\right)  }\left(  v\right)  =\mathfrak{i}%
_{e^{d}\left\vert e\right)  }\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}e_{j}\mathcal{R}%
_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}e_{j}^{d}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}e_{j}^{d}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v^{d}\right)  _{j}=v^{d}.
\end{equation}


\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\left\vert e\right)  }\in\mathcal{B}_{00}\left(
V^{d},V\right)  .`
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{align}
\left\langle v^{d},\mu\right\rangle  &  =\left\langle \mathfrak{i}%
_{e^{d}\left\vert e\right)  }\left(  v\right)  ,\mu\right\rangle =\left\langle
\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}e_{j}^{d}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}%
,\mu\right\rangle =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left\langle e_{j}^{d},\mu\right\rangle
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}e_{j}^{d}\left(
\mu\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left(
\left.  e_{j}^{b}\right\vert \mu\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)
_{j},\,\\
&  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{j}\mathcal{R}%
_{e}\left(  \mu\right)  _{j}=\left(  \left.  v\right\vert \mu\right)
\quad\forall\mu\in\mathcal{V}.
\end{align}


\item
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\left\vert e\right)  }\left(  v\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}\left(  e_{j}^{b}\right\vert \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v^{d}\right)  _{j}.
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\left\langle v^{d},\mu\right\rangle =\left(  \left.  v^{d}\right\vert
\mu\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)
_{j}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \mu\right)  _{j}.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
$\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}e}$ induces a $\mathbb{C}$-symmetric bilinear form
$\left(  \left.  {}\right\vert \right)  _{e}$%
\begin{equation}
\left(  \left.  {}\right\vert \right)  _{e}:\mathcal{V\times V}\rightarrow
\mathbb{C}%
\end{equation}
by
\begin{equation}
\left(  \left.  v\right\vert \mu\right)  _{e}=\left\langle \mathfrak{i}%
_{e^{d}e}\left(  v\right)  ,\mu\right\rangle _{e},\,\forall\mu\in
\mathcal{V},\,v\in\mathcal{V}.
\end{equation}

\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
$\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\sigma_{e}\left(  e\right)  }$ induces a $\mathbb{C}%
$-sesquilinear form $\left\langle \left.  {}\right\vert \right\rangle _{e}$%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left.  {}\right\vert \right\rangle _{e}:\mathcal{V\times
V}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}%
\end{equation}
by
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left.  v\right\vert \mu\right\rangle _{e}=\left\langle
\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\sigma_{e}\left(  e\right)  }\left(  v\right)
,\mu\right\rangle _{e},\,\forall\mu\in\mathcal{V},\,v\in\mathcal{V}.
\end{equation}

\end{definition}

\begin{remark}%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left.  v\right\vert \mu\right\rangle _{e}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}\overline{\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  }_{j}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
\mu\right)  _{j}.
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
$\mathcal{V}$ can be embedded in $\mathcal{V}^{d}$ i.e.
$\mathcal{V\hookrightarrow V}^{d}$ and $\mathcal{W}$ can be embedded in
$\mathcal{W}^{d}$ i.e. $\mathcal{W\hookrightarrow W}^{d}$ thus one can
restrict $M^{d}$ to the embedded subspaces and consider
\begin{equation}
\left.  M^{d}\right\vert _{\mathcal{WV}}:\mathcal{W}\rightarrow\mathcal{V}%
,\quad\left.  M^{d}\right\vert _{\mathcal{WV}}\in\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{W},\mathcal{V}\right)  \hookrightarrow\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{W}^{d},\mathcal{V}^{d}\right)
\end{equation}
and one can define
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{Adjoint} $M^{\dagger}$of $M$ $\in$ $\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{W},\mathcal{V}\right)  $ is defined by
\begin{equation}
M^{\dagger}=\left.  M^{d}\right\vert _{\mathcal{WV}},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\dagger:\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W},\mathcal{V}\right)  \rightarrow
\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W},\mathcal{V}\right)
\end{equation}
i.e.%
\begin{equation}
\dagger\,\in\mathcal{L}_{anti}\left(  \mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W}%
,\mathcal{V}\right)  \right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
A complete normed space is a Banach space.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
In general $\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}e},\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\left\vert e\right)  }$
and $\mathfrak{i}_{e^{d}\left\vert e\right\rangle }$ are not surjective except
for finite dimensional and Hilbert spaces.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}
Let $\mathcal{X}$ and $\mathcal{Y}$ be Banach spaces such that dim$\left\{
\mathcal{X}\right\}  \leq\,$dim$\left\{  \mathcal{Y}\right\}  $ with bases
$\left\{  e_{1},\cdots,e_{q}\right\}  $ and $\left\{  f_{1},\cdots
,f_{q},f_{q+1,\cdots,}f_{p}\right\}  $ then $\mathcal{Y}$ \emph{envelopes
}$\mathcal{X}$ and $\mathcal{X}$ is \emph{embedded }in $\mathcal{Y}$ through
an injection $\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{X}%
,\mathcal{Y}\right)  $
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}:\left\{  e_{1},\cdots,e_{q}\right\}  \rightarrow\left\{
f_{1},\cdots,f_{q},f_{q+1,\cdots,}f_{p}\right\}  ,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}=\mathbb{C}%
\text{-}\operatorname*{linspan}\left\{  f_{q+1,\cdots,}f_{p}\right\}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  =\mathbb{C}%
\text{-}\operatorname*{linspan}\left\{  f_{1},\cdots,f_{q}\right\}  .
\end{equation}

\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
In the proceeding $\mathcal{X\equiv}$ $\mathcal{V}$ and $q=m,$ while
$\mathcal{Y\equiv}$ $\mathcal{W}$ and $p=n$ and general bases for
$\mathcal{V}$ and $\mathcal{W}$ are $\left\{  e_{1},\cdots,e_{m}\right\}  $
and $\left\{  f_{1},\cdots,f_{m},f_{m+1,\cdots,}f_{m}\right\}  .$
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}
A change of basis from $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ to $\left(
\boldsymbol{v}\right)  $ of $\mathcal{V}$ is produced by the map $T_{ve}%
\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  $
\begin{equation}
T_{ve}:\mathcal{V\rightarrow V},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
T_{ve}\left(  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \right)  \rightarrow\left(
\boldsymbol{v}\right)
\end{equation}
is given by
\begin{equation}
\left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)
=\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where $\left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]  $ is defined by the sum of
partial isometries
\begin{equation}
\left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq q}\left[
v_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]
\end{equation}
and its action is given by
\begin{equation}
T_{ve}\left(  e_{k}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq q}\left[  v_{j}e_{j}%
^{d}\right]  \left(  e_{k}\right)  =v_{k}.
\end{equation}

\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}%
\begin{equation}
\left[  w_{j}v_{k}^{d}\right]  ^{\dagger}=\left[  v_{k}w_{j}^{d}\right]
,\,1\leq j\leq m,1\leq k\leq n.
\end{equation}

\end{lemma}

\begin{remark}
$\left[  v_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  $ are partial isometries as
\begin{equation}
\left[  v_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  \left[  v_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  ^{\dagger
}=\left[  v_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  \left[  e_{j}v_{j}^{d}\right]  =\left[
v_{j}v_{j}^{d}\right]  =\left[  v_{j}v_{j}^{d}\right]  ^{2}%
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\left[  v_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  ^{\dagger}\left[  v_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]
=\left[  e_{j}v_{j}^{d}\right]  \left[  v_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  =\left[
e_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  =\left[  e_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  ^{2}.
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Supervector inner products

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]  =\boldsymbol{v}\#\mathbf{e}%
^{dt}=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
v_{1} & \cdots & v_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \#\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[  v_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  \quad
\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  .
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{v\cdot}\mathbf{e}^{dt}=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
v_{1} & \cdots & v_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \cdot\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}v_{j}\left(  e_{j}^{d}\right)  \quad
\in\mathbb{C}%
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\diamond\mathbf{e}^{t}=\left[  \boldsymbol{v}^{d}%
\mathbf{e}^{t}\right]  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
v_{1}^{d} & \cdots & v_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  \diamond\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[  v_{j}^{d}e_{j}\right]  \quad
\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}^{d}\right)  .
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\cdot\mathbf{e}^{t}=\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\left(  \mathbf{e}%
^{t}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
v_{1}^{d} & \cdots & v_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  \cdot\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}v_{j}^{d}\left(  e_{j}\right)  \in\mathbb{C}.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}

\item Supervector outer products%
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{e}^{dt}\#\boldsymbol{v}=\left[  \mathbf{e}^{dt}\boldsymbol{v}\right]
=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  \#\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
v_{1} & \cdots & v_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\left[  e_{1}^{d}v_{1}\right]   & \cdots & \left[  e_{1}^{d}v_{r}\right]  \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
\left[  e_{1}^{d}v_{r}\right]   & \cdots & \left[  e_{r}^{d}v_{r}\right]
\end{array}
\right)  \in\mathcal{M}\left(  r,r,\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)
\right)  \cong\otimes^{r}\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  .
\end{equation}


\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{e}^{dt}\cdot\boldsymbol{v}=\mathbf{e}^{dt}\left(  \boldsymbol{v}%
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  \cdot\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
v_{1} & \cdots & v_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1}^{d}\left(  v_{1}\right)  & \cdots & e_{1}^{d}\left(  v_{r}\right) \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
e_{1}^{d}\left(  v_{r}\right)  & \cdots & e_{r}^{d}\left(  v_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  \in\mathcal{M}\left(  r,r,\mathbb{C}\right)  .
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{e\#}\boldsymbol{v}^{dt}=\left[  \mathbf{e}\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\right]
=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \mathbf{\#}\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
v_{1}^{d} & \cdots & v_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\left[  e_{1}v_{1}^{d}\right]  & \cdots & \left[  e_{1}v_{r}^{d}\right] \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
\left[  e_{1}v_{r}^{d}\right]  & \cdots & \left[  e_{r}v_{r}^{d}\right]
\end{array}
\right)  \in\mathcal{M}\left(  r,r,\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}^{d}\right)
\right)  \cong\otimes^{r}\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}^{d}\right)  .
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{e\cdot}\boldsymbol{v}^{dt}=\mathbf{e}\left(  \boldsymbol{v}%
^{dt}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \cdot\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
v_{1}^{d} & \cdots & v_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1}\left(  v_{1}^{d}\right)  & \cdots & e_{1}\left(  v_{r}^{d}\right) \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
e_{1}\left(  v_{r}^{d}\right)  & \cdots & e_{r}\left(  v_{r}^{d}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  \in\mathcal{M}\left(  r,r,\mathbb{C}\right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The operators $T_{ve}=\left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]
\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  $ are unitary i.e.
\begin{align}
\left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]  ^{\dagger}\left[  \boldsymbol{v}%
\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]   &  =\left[  \mathbf{e}\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\right]
\left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]  =\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{dt}\right]
=I_{\mathcal{V}}\\
\left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]  \left[  \boldsymbol{v}%
\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]  ^{\dagger}  &  =\left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}%
^{d}\right]  \left[  \mathbf{e}\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\right]  =\left[
\boldsymbol{vv}^{dt}\right]  =I_{\mathcal{V}}.
\end{align}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The representation $\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  $ of $T_{ve}$ wrt
the $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $-basis is given by
\begin{equation}
T_{ve}\left(  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \right)  =\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)
\mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  =\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)
\mathcal{M}_{ee^{d}}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where the matrix elements of $\mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  $ are
given by
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  _{jk}=\mathcal{M}_{ee^{d}}\left(
T_{ve}\right)  _{jk}=e_{j}^{d}\left(  T_{ve}\left(  e_{k}\right)  \right)
=e_{j}^{d}\left(  \left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]  \left(
e_{k}\right)  \right)  =e_{j}^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq l\leq q}\left[
v_{l}e_{l}^{d}\right]  \left(  e_{k}\right)  \right)  =e_{j}^{d}\left(
v_{k}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
i.e.%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1}^{d}\left(  v_{1}\right)  & \cdots & e_{1}^{d}\left(  v_{r}\right) \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}\left(  v_{1}\right)  & \cdots & e_{r}^{d}\left(  v_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\left(  v_{1}\right) \\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}\left(  v_{1}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  &
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\cdots\\
\ddots\\
\cdots
\end{array}
& \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\left(  v_{r}\right) \\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}\left(  v_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right)
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v_{1}\right)  & \cdots & \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
v_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The representation $\mathcal{R}_{vv}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  $ of $T_{ve}$ wrt
the $\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)  $-basis is given by
\begin{equation}
T_{ve}\left(  \left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)  \right)  =\left(  \boldsymbol{v}%
\right)  \mathcal{R}_{vv}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  =\left(  \boldsymbol{v}%
\right)  \mathcal{M}_{vv^{d}}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{vv}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  _{jk}=\mathcal{M}_{vv^{d}}\left(
T_{ve}\right)  _{jk}=v_{j}^{d}\left(  T_{ve}\left(  v_{k}\right)  \right)
=v_{j}^{d}\left(  \left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]  \left(
v_{k}\right)  \right)  =v_{j}^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq l\leq q}\left[
v_{l}e_{l}^{d}\right]  \left(  v_{k}\right)  \right)  =e_{j}^{d}\left(
v_{k}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
which shows that the matrix of $T_{ve}=\left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}%
^{d}\right]  $ is the same in the bases $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ and
$\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)  $ that are related to each other by $T_{ve}$
i.e.%
\begin{align}
\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\left(  \left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]  \left(
\boldsymbol{v}\right)  \right)   &  =\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\left(  \boldsymbol{v}%
\right)  \mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)  =\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(
\boldsymbol{v}\right) \\
\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}^{d}\right]  \left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  \right)   &  =\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)
\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  =\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \boldsymbol{v}%
\right)  .
\end{align}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The inverse of $T_{ve}^{-1}=T_{ev}=T_{ve}^{\dagger}$%
\begin{equation}
T_{ve}^{-1}=T_{ev}:\mathcal{V\rightarrow V},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
T_{ev}\left(  \left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)  \right)  \rightarrow\left(
\mathbf{e}\right)
\end{equation}
is given by
\begin{equation}
\left[  \mathbf{e}\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\right]  \left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)
=\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\left[  \mathbf{e}\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\right]  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq q}\left[
e_{j}v_{j}^{d}\right]
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
T_{ev}\left(  v_{k}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq q}\left[  e_{j}v_{j}%
^{d}\right]  \left(  v_{k}\right)  =e_{k}.
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The $\mathcal{R}_{v}$ representation matrix of the inverse is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
v_{1}^{d}\left(  e_{1}\right)  & \cdots & v_{1}^{d}\left(  e_{r}\right) \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
v_{r}^{d}\left(  e_{1}\right)  & \cdots & v_{r}^{d}\left(  e_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
v_{1}^{d}\left(  e_{1}\right) \\
\vdots\\
v_{r}^{d}\left(  e_{1}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  &
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\cdots\\
\ddots\\
\cdots
\end{array}
& \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
v_{1}^{d}\left(  e_{r}\right) \\
\vdots\\
v_{r}^{d}\left(  e_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right)
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  e_{1}\right)  & \cdots & \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(
e_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{lemma}
The inverse $T_{ev}^{-1}$ of $T_{ev}$ is the adjoint $T_{ev}^{\dagger}$ of
$T_{ev}.$
\end{lemma}

\begin{corollary}%
\begin{equation}
v_{j}^{d}\left(  e_{k}\right)  =e_{k}^{\dagger}\left(  v_{j}\right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{corollary}

\begin{example}%
\begin{equation}
e_{k}^{\dagger}\left(  v_{j}\right)  =\left\langle \left.  e_{k}\right\vert
v_{j}\right\rangle .
\end{equation}

\end{example}

\begin{remark}%
\begin{equation}
T_{ev}\left(  \left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)  \right)  =\left(  \boldsymbol{v}%
\right)  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  T_{ev}\right)  =\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)
\mathcal{M}_{vv^{d}}\left(  T_{ev}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{vv}\left(  T_{ev}\right)  _{jk}=\mathcal{M}_{vv^{d}}\left(
T_{ev}\right)  _{jk}=v_{j}^{d}\left(  T_{ev}\left(  v_{k}\right)  \right)
=v_{j}^{d}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{e}\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\right]  \left(
v_{k}\right)  \right)  =v_{j}^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq l\leq q}\left[
e_{l}v_{l}^{d}\right]  \left(  v_{k}\right)  \right)  =v_{j}^{d}\left(
e_{k}\right)  .
\end{equation}
Hence
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}_{vv}\left(  T_{ev}\right)  _{jk}  &  =v_{j}^{d}\left(
e_{k}\right) \\
\mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  _{jk}  &  =e_{j}^{d}\left(
v_{k}\right)  \overset{\text{under certain topological conditions}}{=}%
v_{k}^{d}\left(  e_{j}\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{vv}\left(  T_{ev}\right)  _{kj}.
\end{align}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Matrix elements of the representation are given by
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  T_{ev}\right)  _{jk}  &  =v_{j}^{d}\left(  \left[
\mathbf{e}\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\right]  \left(  v_{k}\right)  \right)  =v_{j}%
^{d}\left(  e_{k}\right) \\
\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  _{jk}^{t}  &  =\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(
T_{ve}\right)  _{kj}=v_{k}^{d}\left(  \left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}%
^{d}\right]  \left(  v_{j}\right)  \right)  =e_{k}^{d}\left(  v_{j}\right)  ,
\end{align}
which gives the same result as
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ev}\right)  _{jk}  &  =e_{j}^{d}\left(  \left[
\mathbf{e}\boldsymbol{v}^{d}\right]  \left(  e_{k}\right)  \right)  =v_{j}%
^{d}\left(  e_{k}\right) \\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ve}\right)  _{jk}^{t}  &  =\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
T_{ve}\right)  _{kj}=e_{k}^{d}\left(  \left[  \boldsymbol{v}\mathbf{e}%
^{d}\right]  \left(  e_{j}\right)  \right)  =e_{k}^{d}\left(  v_{j}\right)  .
\end{align}

\end{remark}

\begin{example}
$\mathcal{X}=\mathbb{C}^{2},\,\mathcal{Y}=\mathbb{C}^{3}$

\begin{enumerate}
\item We consider the mapping $A\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathbb{C}%
^{2},\mathbb{C}^{3}\right)  $
\begin{equation}
Av=w,
\end{equation}
where $v\in\mathbb{C}^{2}$ and $w\in\mathbb{C}^{3}.$

\item The operator $A\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathbb{C}^{2},\mathbb{C}%
^{3}\right)  $ and vectors $v\in\mathbb{C}^{2}$ have the representations
$\mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  A\right)  $ and $\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  $
wrt the $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ basis given by
\begin{equation}
A\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  A\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  A\right)
_{12} & \mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  A\right)  _{13}\\
\mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  A\right)
_{22} & \mathcal{R}_{ee}\left(  A\right)  _{23}%
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}
and%
\begin{equation}
v\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  ,
\end{equation}
while the vector $w\in\mathbb{C}^{3}$ has the representation $\mathcal{R}%
_{f}\left(  w\right)  $ wrt the $\left(  \mathbf{f}\right)  $ basis given by
\begin{equation}
w\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  w\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  w\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  w\right)  _{2}\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  w\right)  _{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}


\item One can use the resolution of the identities
\begin{equation}
\left[  \mathbf{ff}^{d}\right]  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq3}\left[  f_{j}f_{j}%
^{d}\right]  =I_{\mathbb{C}^{3}}%
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq3}\left[  e_{j}e_{j}%
^{d}\right]  =I_{\mathbb{C}^{2}}%
\end{equation}
to obtain
\begin{align}
Av  &  =\left[  \mathbf{ff}^{d}\right]  A\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{dt}\right]
v=\left(  \mathbf{f}\right)  \left(  \mathbf{f}^{d}\left(  A\mathbf{e}\right)
\right)  \mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  v\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
f_{1}^{d}\\
f_{2}^{d}\\
f_{3}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  A\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
e_{2}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  v\\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
f_{1}^{d}\left(  Ae_{1}\right)  & f_{1}^{d}\left(  Ae_{2}\right) \\
f_{2}^{d}\left(  Ae_{1}\right)  & f_{2}^{d}\left(  Ae_{2}\right) \\
f_{3}^{d}\left(  Ae_{1}\right)  & f_{3}^{d}\left(  Ae_{2}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{12}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{22}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{32}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{align}


\item In more detail
\begin{align}
Av  &  =A\left(  e_{1}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{1}+e_{2}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{2}\right)  =Ae_{1}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
v\right)  _{1}+Ae_{2}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{2}=\left(
Ae_{1}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{1}+\left(  Ae_{2}\right)
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{2}\\
&  =\left(  \overset{}{f_{1}\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11}%
+f_{2}\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{21}+f_{3}\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(
A\right)  _{31}}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{1}+\left(
\overset{}{f_{1}\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{12}+f_{2}\mathcal{R}%
_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{22}+f_{3}\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{32}%
}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{2}\\
&  =\left(  f_{1}\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
v\right)  _{1}+f_{2}\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{21}\mathcal{R}%
_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{1}+f_{3}\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{31}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{1}\right) \\
&  +\left(  f_{1}\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{12}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
v\right)  _{2}+f_{2}\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{22}\mathcal{R}%
_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{2}+f_{3}\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{32}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{2}\right) \\
&  =f_{1}\left(  \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
v\right)  _{1}+\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{12}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
v\right)  _{2}\right)  +f_{2}\left(  \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{21}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{1}+\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{22}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{2}\right) \\
&  +f_{3}\left(  \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{31}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
v\right)  _{1}+\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{32}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
v\right)  _{2}\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)
_{1}+\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{12}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)
_{2}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{21}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)
_{1}+\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{22}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)
_{2}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{31}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)
_{1}+\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{32}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)
_{2}%
\end{array}
\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{12}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{22}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{32}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  _{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(  \mathbf{f}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  v\right)  .
\end{align}


\item The operator $A\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathbb{C}^{2},\mathbb{C}%
^{3}\right)  $ can be expanded as
\begin{align}
A  &  =\left[  \mathbf{ff}^{d}\right]  A\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]
=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
f_{1}^{d}\\
f_{2}^{d}\\
f_{3}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  A\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
e_{2}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(  \mathbf{f}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  \left(
\mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  =\left[  \mathbf{fe}^{dt}\right]  \mathcal{R}%
_{fe}\left(  A\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
f_{1}^{d}\left(  Ae_{1}\right)  & f_{1}^{d}\left(  Ae_{2}\right) \\
f_{2}^{d}\left(  Ae_{1}\right)  & f_{2}^{d}\left(  Ae_{2}\right) \\
f_{3}^{d}\left(  Ae_{1}\right)  & f_{3}^{d}\left(  Ae_{2}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
e_{2}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{12}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{22}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{32}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
e_{2}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right) \\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j\leq3\\1\leq k\leq2}}f_{j}\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(
A\right)  _{jk}e_{k}^{d}=\sum_{\substack{1\leq j\leq3\\1\leq k\leq2}}\left[
f_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{jk}.
\end{align}


\item The injection of $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ into $\left(
\mathbf{f}\right)  $ denoted by $\mathfrak{i}_{fe}$ embeds $\mathcal{X}%
=\mathbb{C}^{2}$ into $\mathcal{Y}=\mathbb{C}^{3}$ has the following properties

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{gather}
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}:\mathbb{C}^{2}\rightarrow\mathbb{C}^{3}\\
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}:\ker\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}\subseteq
\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}
\subseteq\mathcal{Y}\\
\ker\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}\cong\operatorname{Ran}\left\{
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  .
\end{gather}


\item by \emph{definition} $\mathfrak{i}_{fe}$ transforms the basis $\left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  $ of $\mathcal{X}$ into the basis $\left(  \mathbf{f}%
\right)  $ of $\mathcal{Y}$
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}:\left\{  e_{1},e_{2}\right\}  \rightarrow\left\{
f_{1},f_{2},f_{3}\right\}  =\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{1}\right)
,\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{2}\right)  ,f_{3}\right\}  ,\quad f_{3}%
\in\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}%
\end{equation}
i.e. embeds the basis $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ into $\mathcal{Y}$ and

\item can be expanded as
\begin{align}
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}  &  =\left[  \mathbf{ff}^{d}\right]  \mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
f_{1}^{d}\\
f_{2}^{d}\\
f_{3}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
e_{2}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(  \mathbf{f}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\right)  \left(  \mathbf{e}^{dt}\right)  =\left[  \mathbf{fe}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
f_{1}^{d}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{1}\right)  \right)  & f_{1}%
^{d}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{2}\right)  \right) \\
f_{2}^{d}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{1}\right)  \right)  & f_{2}%
^{d}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{2}\right)  \right) \\
f_{3}^{d}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{1}\right)  \right)  & f_{3}%
^{d}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{2}\right)  \right)
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
e_{2}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}%
_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  _{12}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}%
_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  _{22}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}%
_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  _{32}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
e_{2}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
f_{1} & f_{2} & f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
e_{2}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left[  f_{1}e_{1}^{d}\right]  +\left[  f_{2}e_{2}^{d}\right]  ,
\end{align}
where

\item the representation of $\mathfrak{i}_{fe}$ wrt the $\left(
\mathbf{f}\right)  $ and $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ bases is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}%
_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  _{12}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}%
_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  _{22}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}%
_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  _{32}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}


\item the action of $\mathfrak{i}_{fe}$ on the basis $\left(  \mathbf{e}%
\right)  $
\begin{align}
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \right)   &
=\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{1}\right)  & \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{2}\right)
& 0
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{1}\right)  & \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{2}\right)
& f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{1}\right)  & \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{2}\right)
& f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  ,
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  $ is the $\left(
\mathbf{f},\mathbf{e}\right)  $-representation of the injection $\mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}$ and $\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  $ is the $\left(
\mathbf{f}\right)  $-representation of $A\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}=A\mathfrak{i}%
_{ef}$.

\begin{description}
\item[*] Explicitly the $\left(  \mathbf{f},\mathbf{e}\right)  $-representaion
$\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  $ of the injection $\equiv$
embedding $\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\in$ $\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{X}%
,\mathcal{Y}\right)  $ is given as
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}
and the

\item[*] representaion $\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  $ of
the image of the injection $\equiv$ embedding $\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\in$
$\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y}\right)  $ considered as an
operator belonging to $\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{Y}\right)  $ wrt the basis
$\left(  \mathbf{f}\right)  $ is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{description}

\item Embedding of vectors $z\in\mathcal{X}$ into $\mathcal{Y}$
\begin{align}
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  z\right)   &  =\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(
\overset{}{\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  }\right)  =\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \right)  =\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \right)  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{1}\right)  & \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{2}\right)
& f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{2}\\
0
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{1}\right)  & \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{2}\right)
& f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  z\right)  _{2}\\
0
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{align}


\begin{description}
\item[*] where $\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  $ is the representaion of the
vector $z\in$ $\mathcal{X}$ wrt the basis $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ i.e.
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{2}%
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}
and

\item[*] $\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  z\right)  \right)  $
is the representaion of the image $\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  z\right)
\in\mathcal{Y}$ of the vector $z\in$ $\mathcal{X}$ wrt the basis $\left(
\mathbf{f}\right)  $ i.e.
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  z\right)  \right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  z\right)  _{2}\\
0
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{2}\\
0
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{description}

\item Representation of the injection process in $\mathcal{Y}$
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  z\right)  \right)
=\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  \overset{}{\left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  }\right)  \right)
=\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
e_{1} & e_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \right)  \right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  z\right)  _{2}\\
0
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}


\item Embedding correspondence of vectors%
\begin{equation}
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  z\right)  _{2}\\
0
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{2}\\
0
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}


\item The \emph{generalized} inverse $\mathfrak{i}_{ef}^{-1}=$ $\mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}$ of the injection $\mathfrak{i}_{ef}$
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{ef}^{-1}=\mathfrak{i}_{fe}:\operatorname{Ran}\left\{
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  \rightarrow\mathcal{X}%
\end{equation}


\item $f$-representation of the injection $\mathfrak{i}_{fe}$%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}


\item $e$-representation of the injection $\mathfrak{i}_{fe}$%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}


\item $\mathfrak{i}_{fe},\mathfrak{i}_{ef},\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}%
=\mathfrak{i}_{ef}$ and $\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}=\mathfrak{i}_{ef}$ and their
representations are partal isometries i.e.
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)   &  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1
\end{array}
\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  I_{2}\right) \\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}\right)   &  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  P_{\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\right\}  }\right)  .
\end{align}


\item Projections onto Range and the compliment of the kernal
\begin{gather}
P_{\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  }=\mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\mathfrak{i}_{ef}=\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{\dagger
}:\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  \rightarrow
\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\} \\
\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  \subseteq\mathcal{Y}\\
P_{\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}}=\mathfrak{i}%
_{ef}\mathfrak{i}_{fe}=\mathfrak{i}_{ef}\mathfrak{i}_{ef}^{\dagger
}:\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}\rightarrow
\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}\\
\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}\subseteq
\mathcal{X}.
\end{gather}


\item Representations of the projectors and injections
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}_{ef}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{ef}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)   &  =\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \mathfrak{i}%
_{ef}\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1
\end{array}
\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  P_{\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}}\right) \\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{ef}\left(
\mathfrak{i}_{ef}\right)   &  =\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\mathfrak{i}_{ef}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  P_{\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\right\}  }\right)  .
\end{align}


\item Using resolution of identities one obtains
\begin{align}
\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]  \left(  \mathfrak{i}_{ef}\mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\right)  \left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]   &  =\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)
\left(  \overset{}{\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{ef}\mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\right)  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  }\right)  \left(  \mathbf{e}%
^{d}\right)  ^{t}=\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \left(  \overset{}{\left(
\mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  \left(  \mathfrak{i}_{ef}\left(  \mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \right)  \right)  }\right)  \left(
\mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  ^{t}=\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \left(
\overset{}{\left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  \left(  \mathfrak{i}_{ef}\left(
\mathbf{f}\right)  \right)  }\right)  \left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  ^{t}\\
&  =\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \left(  \overset{}{\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  }\right)  \left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  ^{t}%
=P_{\ker\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}}%
\end{align}
and
\begin{align}
\left[  \mathbf{ff}^{dt}\right]  \left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\mathfrak{i}%
_{ef}\right)  \left[  \mathbf{ff}^{dt}\right]   &  =\left(  \mathbf{f}\right)
\left(  \overset{}{\mathbf{f}^{d}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{ef}\mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\right)  \left(  \mathbf{f}\right)  }\right)  \left(  \mathbf{f}%
^{d}\right)  ^{t}=\left(  \mathbf{f}\right)  \left(  \overset{}{\mathbf{f}%
^{d}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{ef}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  \mathbf{f}%
\right)  \right)  \right)  }\right)  \left(  \mathbf{f}^{d}\right)
^{t}=\left(  \mathbf{f}\right)  \left(  \overset{}{\mathbf{f}^{d}\left(
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \right)  }\right)  \left(
\mathbf{f}^{d}\right)  ^{t}\\
&  =\left(  \mathbf{f}\right)  \left(  \overset{}{\mathbf{f}^{d}\left(
\mathbf{f}\right)  }\right)  \left(  \mathbf{f}^{d}\right)  ^{t}%
=P_{\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{ef}\right\}  }.
\end{align}


\item Decomposition of $A\ $%
\begin{align}
A  &  =P_{\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  }%
AP_{\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}}=\left[
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \left(  \overset{}{\mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  }\right)  ^{d}\right]  A\left[  \left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  ^{d}\right] \\
&  =\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \left(  \overset{}{\left(
\overset{}{\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  }\right)  ^{d}\left(
A\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \right)  }\right)  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)
^{d}=\sum_{\substack{1\leq j\leq3\\1\leq k\leq2}}\left[  \left(
\overset{}{\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{j}\right)  }\right)  ^{d}e_{k}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{jk}%
\end{align}


\item Matrix elements of $A\ $%
\begin{equation}
\left(  \overset{}{\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  ^{d}\left(
A\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \right)  }\right)  _{jk}=\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(
e_{j}\right)  ^{d}\left(  \overset{}{Ae_{k}}\right)  =\mathcal{M}_{fe}\left(
A\right)  _{jk}=\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{jk}=f_{j}^{d}\left(
Ae_{j}\right)  ,\quad1\leq j\leq3,1\leq k\leq2
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{gather}
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(
P_{\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  }%
AP_{\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}}\right)
=\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  P_{\operatorname{Ran}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}%
_{fe}\right\}  }\right)  \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  \mathcal{R}%
_{e}\left(  P_{\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right\}  ^{c}%
}\right) \\
=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0\\
0 & 0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)
_{12}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)
_{22}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)
_{32}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)
_{12}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)
_{22}\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1
\end{array}
\right) \\
=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)
_{12}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)
_{22}\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  ,
\end{gather}
i.e.%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  =\mathcal{M}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{12}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{22}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{32}%
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}


\item Embedding of $A\ $%
\begin{equation}
\left(  A\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  :\mathcal{Y\rightarrow Y}.
\end{equation}


\item The matrix of the embedded operator $A$ i.e. of the corresponding
operator $A\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}=A\mathfrak{i}_{ef}$
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  A\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)   &  =\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(
A\right)  \mathcal{R}_{ef}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{12}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{22}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{32}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0
\end{array}
\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{12} & 0\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{22} & 0\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{32} & 0
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  A\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}%
_{f}\left(  A\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}\right)  _{12} & 0\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  A\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}%
_{f}\left(  A\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}\right)  _{22} & 0\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  A\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}%
_{f}\left(  A\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}\right)  _{32} & 0
\end{array}
\right)  ,
\end{align}
where the

\begin{enumerate}
\item[*] inverse
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}=\mathfrak{i}_{ef}.
\end{equation}


\item[*] representations
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}_{ef}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{ef}\right)   &  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & 1 & 0
\end{array}
\right) \\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\right)   &  =\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(
\mathfrak{i}_{ef}^{-1}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
1 & 0\\
0 & 1\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{align}

\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

\item Representations of the transformation $Az=\eta\ $

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{12}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{22}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{32}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \eta\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \eta\right)  _{2}\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \eta\right)  _{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \eta\right)
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\eta=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{1}\right)  & \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{2}\right)
& f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \eta\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{1}\right)  & \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  e_{2}\right)
& f_{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{12}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{22}\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{32}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right)  _{2}%
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}


\begin{description}
\item[*] The embedded $\equiv$ corresponding transformation%
\begin{equation}
\left(  A_{fe}\mathfrak{i}_{ef}\right)  \left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(
z\right)  \right)  =\left(  A\mathfrak{i}_{fe}^{-1}\right)  \left(
\mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  z\right)  \right)  =Az
\end{equation}


\item[*] has the representation
\begin{align}
&  \left.  \qquad\qquad\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  A\mathfrak{i}_{ef}\right)
\right.  \qquad\qquad\quad\quad\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(
z\right)  \right)  \qquad\quad=\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  z\right) \\
&  \qquad\qquad\qquad\parallel\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad\qquad\parallel
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad\parallel\qquad\qquad\\
&  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{11} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{12} & 0\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{21} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{22} & 0\\
\mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)  _{31} & \mathcal{R}_{fe}\left(  A\right)
_{32} & 0
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  z\right)  \right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \mathfrak{i}_{fe}\left(  z\right)  \right)  _{2}\\
0
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \eta\right)  _{1}\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \eta\right)  _{2}\\
\mathcal{R}_{f}\left(  \eta\right)  _{3}%
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{align}

\end{description}
\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
\end{example}

\begin{definition}
One can define an adjoint $A^{\dagger}$ of a closed densely defined operator
$A\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  $ produced by the adjoint
operation "$\dagger"\in\mathcal{L}_{anti}\left(  \mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{V}\right)  \right)  $
\end{definition}

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\left(  zA\right)  ^{\dagger}=\overline{z}A^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
A^{\dagger\dagger}=A.
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\left(  A+B\right)  ^{\dagger}=A^{\dagger}+B^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
A^{\dagger}B^{\dagger}=\left(  BA\right)  ^{\dagger}\text{ on }%
\operatorname*{Dom}\left\{  A^{\dagger}\right\}  \cap\operatorname*{Dom}%
\left\{  B^{\dagger}\right\}  .
\end{equation}


\item If $A\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  ,$ then
\begin{equation}
\Vert A^{\dagger}A\Vert_{\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  }\leq M\Vert
A\Vert_{\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  }^{2}.
\end{equation}


\item If $A\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  ,$ it has a bounded
extension to $\mathcal{L}\left(  [\mathcal{V}]\right)  ,$ where $[\mathcal{V}%
]$ is a Hilbert space containing $\mathcal{V}.$
\end{enumerate}

\begin{remark}%
\begin{gather}
\mathcal{V}\overset{M}{------\rightarrow}\mathcal{W}\\
\left\vert \qquad\quad\qquad\qquad\qquad\right\vert \\
\digamma_{v}\left\vert \qquad\quad\quad\quad\qquad\,\,\qquad\right\vert
\digamma_{w}\\
\downarrow\quad\qquad\qquad\quad\qquad\downarrow\,\\
\mathcal{V}^{d}\overset{M^{d}}{\longleftarrow------}\mathcal{W}^{d}%
\end{gather}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
One has dual "bases"
\begin{align}
v_{j},v_{j}^{d},\quad1  &  \leq j\leq m\\
w_{j},w_{j}^{d},\quad1  &  \leq j\leq n,
\end{align}
where
\begin{equation}
\dim\left\{  \mathcal{V}\right\}  =m\text{ and }\dim\left\{  \mathcal{W}%
\right\}  =n.
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
In the following $1\leq j\leq m,1\leq k\leq n.$One can form the
\emph{following injections from the tensor product space} $\mathcal{W\otimes
V}^{d}$ to the operator space $\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}%
\right)  :$

\begin{enumerate}
\item The tensor product space $\mathcal{W\otimes V}^{d}$
\begin{equation}
\Xi_{wv^{d}}:\mathcal{W\otimes V}^{d}\rightarrow\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}\right)
\end{equation}
the operator space is generated by the elementary operators $\left\{  \left[
w_{j}v_{k}^{d}\right]  \right\}  .$

\item The tensor product space $\mathcal{V\otimes W}^{d}$
\begin{equation}
\Xi_{wv^{d}}:\mathcal{V\otimes W}^{d}\rightarrow\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{W},\mathcal{V}\right)
\end{equation}
the operator space is generated by the elementary operators $\left\{  \left[
v_{j}w_{k}^{d}\right]  \right\}  =\left\{  \left[  w_{j}v_{k}^{d}\right]
^{d}\right\}  .$

\item The tensor product space $\mathcal{V}^{d}\mathcal{\otimes W}$%
\begin{equation}
\Xi_{v^{d}w}:\mathcal{V}^{d}\mathcal{\otimes W}\rightarrow\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{W}^{d},\mathcal{V}^{d}\right)
\end{equation}
the operator space is generated by the elementary operators $\left\{  \left[
v_{j}^{d}w_{k}\right]  \right\}  .$

\item The tensor product space $\mathcal{W}^{d}\mathcal{\otimes V}$%
\begin{equation}
\Xi_{w^{d}v}:\mathcal{W}^{d}\mathcal{\otimes V}\rightarrow\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{V}^{d},\mathcal{W}^{d}\right)
\end{equation}
the operator space is generated by the elementary operators $\left\{  \left[
w_{j}^{d}v_{k}\right]  \right\}  =\left\{  \left[  v_{j}^{d}w_{k}\right]
^{d}\right\}  .$

\item The tensor product space $\mathcal{W\otimes W}^{d}$%
\begin{equation}
\Xi_{ww^{d}}:\mathcal{W\otimes W}^{d}\rightarrow\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{W},\mathcal{W}\right)
\end{equation}
the operator space is generated by the elementary operators $\left\{  \left[
w_{j}w_{k}^{d}\right]  \right\}  .$

\item The tensor product space $\mathcal{W}^{d}\mathcal{\otimes W}$%
\begin{equation}
\Xi_{w^{d}w}:\mathcal{W}^{d}\mathcal{\otimes W}\rightarrow\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{W}^{d},\mathcal{W}^{d}\right)
\end{equation}
the operator space is generated by the elementary operators $\left\{  \left[
w_{j}^{d}w_{k}\right]  \right\}  =\left\{  \left[  w_{j}w_{k}^{d}\right]
^{d}\right\}  .$

\item The tensor product space $\mathcal{V\otimes V}^{d}$%
\begin{equation}
\Xi_{vv^{d}}:\mathcal{V\otimes V}^{d}\rightarrow\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{V},\mathcal{V}\right)
\end{equation}
the operator space is generated by the elementary operators $\left\{  \left[
v_{j}v_{k}^{d}\right]  \right\}  .$

\item The tensor product space $\mathcal{V}^{d}\mathcal{\otimes V}$%
\begin{equation}
\Xi_{v^{d}w}:\mathcal{V}^{d}\mathcal{\otimes V}\rightarrow\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{V}^{d},\mathcal{V}^{d}\right)
\end{equation}
the operator space is generated by the elementary operators $\left\{  \left[
v_{j}^{d}v_{k}\right]  \right\}  =\left\{  \left[  v_{j}v_{k}^{d}\right]
^{d}\right\}  .$

\item One has the injections

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\digamma_{v}:\mathcal{V}\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{d}%
\end{equation}
and

\item
\begin{equation}
\digamma_{v^{d}}^{-1}:\operatorname*{Range}\left\{  \digamma_{v}\right\}
\rightarrow\mathcal{V}.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}

\item One has the injections

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\digamma_{w}:\mathcal{W}\rightarrow\mathcal{W}^{d}%
\end{equation}
and

\item
\begin{equation}
\digamma_{w^{d}}^{-1}:\operatorname*{Range}\left\{  \digamma_{w}\right\}
\rightarrow\mathcal{W},
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}

\item leading to
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\digamma_{vw}}:\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}\right)
\rightarrow\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W}^{d},\mathcal{V}^{d}\right)
\end{equation}
by

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
A^{d}=\widehat{\digamma_{vw}}\left(  A\right)  =\digamma_{w}\circ
A\circ\digamma_{v^{d}}^{-1}%
\end{equation}
and its inverse

\item
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\digamma_{vw}^{-1}}:\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W}^{d},\mathcal{V}%
^{d}\right)  \rightarrow\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}\right)
\end{equation}
by

\item
\begin{equation}
A=\widehat{\digamma_{vw}^{-1}}\left(  A^{d}\right)  =\digamma_{w}^{-1}\circ
A^{d}\circ\digamma_{v^{d}}.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
In the case $\digamma_{v}=\digamma_{\left\vert v\right\rangle }$ and
$\digamma_{w}=\digamma_{\left\vert w\right\rangle }$ one has for
$A\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}\right)  $
\begin{equation}
A^{\dagger}=\widehat{\digamma_{\left\vert vw\right\rangle }}\left(  A\right)
,
\end{equation}
where $A^{\dagger}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W},\mathcal{V}\right)  .$
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
In finite dimensions $\mathcal{W}\cong\mathcal{W}^{d}$ and $\mathcal{V}%
\cong\mathcal{V}^{d}$ and if they are Hilbert spaces one can use $\digamma
_{e}=\digamma_{\left\vert e\right\rangle }$ and consider $\mathcal{W}%
=\mathcal{W}^{d}$ and $\mathcal{V}=\mathcal{V}^{d}.$
\end{remark}

One can always decompose $\mathcal{V}$ as the direct sum
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{V}=\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  M\right\}  \oplus\operatorname{Ker}%
\left\{  M\right\}  ^{c}%
\end{equation}
and $\mathcal{W}$ as the direct sum
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{W}=\operatorname*{Range}\left\{  M\right\}  \oplus
\operatorname*{Range}\left\{  M\right\}  ^{c}%
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
M:\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  M\right\}  ^{c}\rightarrow\operatorname*{Range}%
\left\{  M\right\}  \subseteq\mathcal{W}%
\end{equation}
is an injection.

If

\begin{enumerate}
\item $\dim\left\{  \mathcal{V}\right\}  <\dim\left\{  \mathcal{W}\right\}  $
then
\begin{equation}
\operatorname*{Range}\left\{  M\right\}  \subset\mathcal{W}%
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  M\right\}  \supseteq\phi.
\end{equation}


\item $\dim\left\{  \mathcal{V}\right\}  >\dim\left\{  \mathcal{W}\right\}  $
then%
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  M\right\}  \supsetneqq\phi
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
M:\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  M\right\}  ^{c}\rightarrow\mathcal{W}%
\end{equation}
is an injection but may or may not be a surjection i.e.
\begin{equation}
\operatorname*{Range}\left\{  M\right\}  \subseteq\mathcal{W}.
\end{equation}


\item $\dim\left\{  \mathcal{V}\right\}  =\dim\left\{  \mathcal{W}\right\}  $
then
\begin{equation}
M:\operatorname{Ker}\left\{  M\right\}  ^{c}\rightarrow\mathcal{W}%
\end{equation}
is an injection and
\begin{equation}
\operatorname*{Range}\left\{  M\right\}  \subseteq\mathcal{W}.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}

\subparagraph{Singular Values}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{non-negative} real number $\U{3c3} _{k}$ is a \emph{singular value}
for $M\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}\right)  $ \emph{if and
only if} there exist unit-length vectors $\left\{  \left.  u_{k}\right\vert
1\leq k\leq p=\min(m,n)\right\}  $ in $\mathcal{W}$ and $\left\{  \left.
z_{k}\right\vert 1\leq k\leq p=\min(m,n)\right\}  $ in $\mathcal{V}$ defined
by
\begin{equation}
Mz_{k}=\U{3c3} _{k}u_{k},\quad1\leq k\leq p=\min(m,n)
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
M^{\dagger}u_{k}=\U{3c3} _{k}z_{k},\quad1\leq k\leq p=\min(m,n),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\dim\left\{  \mathcal{V}\right\}  =n
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\dim\left\{  \mathcal{W}\right\}  =m.
\end{equation}

\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
\emph{Column Operators}:
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{C}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq m}\left[  w_{j}%
w_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{jk}\in\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{W}\right)  ,\quad1\leq k\leq p=\min(m,n)
\end{equation}
and%
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{C}_{v}\left(  Z^{\dagger}\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq n}\left[
v_{j}v_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  Z^{\dagger}\right)  _{jk}%
\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  ,\quad1\leq k\leq p=\min(m,n).
\end{equation}

\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
The vectors $u_{k}$ and $z_{k}$ are called \emph{left-singular} and
\emph{right-singular} vectors for the singular values $\U{3c3} _{k}$,
respectively. The first $p=\min(m,n)$ \emph{column operators} of the unitary
operators $U\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  $ and $Z\in
\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W}\right)  $ correspond to \emph{left- and
right-singular vectors corresponding to non zero singular values}.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
In the case of a Hilbert space one has
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{C}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq p}\left\vert
w_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle w_{k}\right\vert \mathcal{R}_{w}\left(
u_{k}\right)  _{j}=\left\vert u_{k}\right\rangle \left\langle w_{k}\right\vert
=\sum_{1\leq j\leq p}\left[  w_{j}w_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{w}\left(
U\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq p}\left[  w_{j}w_{k}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  u_{k}\right)  _{j}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq p}\left[
w_{j}w_{k}^{d}\right]  w_{j}^{d}\left(  u_{k}\right)  =\left[  u_{k}w_{k}%
^{d}\right]  .
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathfrak{C}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{k}$ is the operator corresponding
to the $k^{th}$ column of the operator $U$ in the representation
$\mathcal{R}_{w}$ which produces the matrix representation $\mathcal{R}%
_{w}\left(  \mathfrak{C}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{k}\right)  $ of $\mathfrak{C}%
_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{k}$ that has column matrix elements
\begin{gather}
\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  \mathfrak{C}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{k}\right)
_{\alpha\beta}=w_{\alpha}^{d}\left(  \mathfrak{C}_{w}\left(  U\right)
_{k}\left(  w_{\beta}\right)  \right)  =w_{\alpha}^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq
j\leq m}\left[  w_{j}w_{k}^{d}\right]  \left(  w_{\beta}\right)
\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{jk}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq m}w_{\alpha
}^{d}\left(  w_{j}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{jk}\delta_{\beta
k}=\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{\alpha k}\delta_{\beta k}\\
1\leq\alpha\leq m,\quad1\leq\beta=k\leq p
\end{gather}
i.e. only the $k^{th}$ column of $\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  \mathfrak{C}%
_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{k}\right)  $ is non zero and is constituted by matrix
elements $\left\{  \mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{\alpha k},1\leq
\alpha\leq m\right\}  $ that are the representation coefficients $\left\{
\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  u_{k}\right)  _{\alpha},1\leq\alpha\leq m\right\}  $ of
the vector $u_{k}$ in the representation $\mathcal{R}_{w},$ i.e.
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{\alpha k}=\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(
u_{k}\right)  _{\alpha},1\leq\alpha\leq m
\end{equation}
and

\item
\begin{gather}
w_{\alpha}^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq m}\left[  w_{j}w_{k}^{d}\right]
\left(  w_{\beta}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{jk}\right)
=w_{\alpha}^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq m}\left[  w_{j}w_{j}^{d}\right]
u_{k}\right)  =w_{\alpha}^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq m}w_{j}w_{j}^{d}\left(
u_{k}\right)  \right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq m}w_{\alpha}^{d}\left(
w_{j}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  u_{k}\right)  _{j}=\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(
u_{k}\right)  _{\alpha}\\
1\leq\alpha\leq m,\quad1\leq\beta=k\leq p.
\end{gather}


\item The unitary operator $U$ can be expressed as a sum of its associated
column opeators i.e.
\begin{equation}
U=\sum_{1\leq k\leq m}\mathfrak{C}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{k}%
\end{equation}
as
\begin{equation}
U=\sum_{1\leq k\leq m}\mathfrak{C}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq k\leq
m}\sum_{1\leq j\leq m}\left[  w_{j}w_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{w}\left(
U\right)  _{jk}.
\end{equation}


\item The representation $\mathcal{R}_{u}\left(  U\right)  $ in the singular
vector basis $\left\{  \left.  u_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq m\right\}  $ of
$\mathcal{W}$ is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{u}\left(  U\right)  =\mathbb{I}_{m},
\end{equation}
which is consistent with the expansion
\begin{equation}
U=\sum_{1\leq k\leq m}\mathfrak{C}_{u}\left(  U\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq k\leq
m}\sum_{1\leq j\leq m}\left[  u_{j}u_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{u}\left(
U\right)  _{jk}.
\end{equation}


\item $\left\{  u_{k},1\leq k\leq m\right\}  $ are degenerate eigenvectors of
$U$ associated with eigenvale $+1$
\begin{equation}
Uu_{k}=u_{k},\quad1\leq k\leq m
\end{equation}


\item $\mathfrak{C}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  _{k}$ is the operator corresponding
to the $k^{th}$ column of the operator $Z$ in the representation
$\mathcal{R}_{\nu}$ and the matrix representation $\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(
\mathfrak{C}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  _{k}\right)  $ of $\mathfrak{C}_{v}\left(
V\right)  _{k}$ has column matrix elements
\begin{gather}
\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  \mathfrak{C}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  _{k}\right)
_{\alpha\beta}=v_{\alpha}^{d}\left(  \mathfrak{C}_{v}\left(  Z\right)
_{k}\left(  v_{\beta}\right)  \right)  =v_{\alpha}^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq
j\leq m}\left[  v_{j}v_{k}^{d}\right]  \left(  v_{\beta}\right)
\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  _{jk}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq m}v_{\alpha
}^{d}\left(  v_{j}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  _{jk}\delta_{\beta
k}=\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  _{\alpha k}\delta_{\beta k}\\
1\leq\alpha\leq n,\quad1\leq\beta=k\leq p
\end{gather}
i.e. only the $k^{th}$ column of $\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  \mathfrak{C}%
_{v}\left(  Z\right)  _{k}\right)  $ is non zero, which is constituted by
matrix elements $\left\{  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  _{\alpha k}%
,1\leq\alpha\leq n\right\}  $ that are the representation coefficients
$\left\{  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  z_{k}\right)  _{\alpha},1\leq\alpha\leq
\beta\right\}  $ of the vector $z_{k}$ in the representation $\mathcal{R}%
_{v},$ i.e.
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  _{\alpha k}=\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(
z_{k}\right)  _{\alpha},1\leq\alpha\leq n
\end{equation}
and

\item
\begin{gather}
v_{\alpha}^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq m}\left[  v_{j}v_{k}^{d}\right]
\left(  v_{\beta}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  _{jk}\right)
=v_{\alpha}^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq n}\left[  v_{j}v_{j}^{d}\right]
v_{k}\right)  =v_{\alpha}^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq n}v_{j}v_{j}^{d}\left(
z_{k}\right)  \right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq m}v_{\alpha}^{d}\left(
v_{j}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  z_{k}\right)  _{j}=\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(
z_{k}\right)  _{\alpha}\\
1\leq\alpha\leq n,\quad1\leq\beta=k\leq p.
\end{gather}


\item The unitary operator $U$ can be expressed as a sum of its associated
column operators i.e.
\begin{equation}
Z=\sum_{1\leq k\leq m}\mathfrak{C}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  _{k}%
\end{equation}
as
\begin{equation}
Z=\sum_{1\leq k\leq n}\mathfrak{C}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq k\leq
m}\sum_{1\leq j\leq m}\left[  v_{j}v_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(
Z\right)  _{jk}.
\end{equation}


\item The representation $\mathcal{R}_{z}\left(  Z\right)  $ in the singular
vector basis $\left\{  \left.  z_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq n\right\}  $ of
$\mathcal{V}$ is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{z}\left(  Z\right)  =\mathbb{I}_{n},
\end{equation}
which is consistent with the expansion
\begin{equation}
Z=\sum_{1\leq k\leq n}\mathfrak{C}_{z}\left(  Z\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq k\leq
m}\sum_{1\leq j\leq m}\left[  z_{j}z_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{z}\left(
Z\right)  _{jk}.
\end{equation}


\item $\left\{  z_{k},1\leq k\leq n\right\}  $ are degenerate eigenvectors of
$Z$ associated with eigenvale $+1$ i.e.
\begin{equation}
Zz_{k}=z_{k},\quad1\leq k\leq n.
\end{equation}


\item In the case of Hilbert spaces the unitary change of basis from $\left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  $ to $\left(  \mathbf{f}\right)  $ produced by the operator
$T_{fe}$ given by
\begin{equation}
T_{fe}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left\vert f_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{j}\right\vert
\end{equation}
and the column operators are
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{C}_{e}\left(  T_{fe}\right)  _{k}=\left\vert f_{k}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{k}\right\vert ,\,1\leq k\leq r
\end{equation}
as
\begin{equation}
\left\langle e_{p}\right\vert \left(  \left\vert f_{k}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{k}\right\vert \right)  \left\vert e_{q}\right\rangle
=\left\langle \left.  e_{p}\right\vert f_{k}\right\rangle \delta_{kq},\,1\leq
p,q\leq r,\,1\leq k\leq r.
\end{equation}


\item The columns $\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  f_{k}\right)  ,\,1\leq k\leq r$ of
$\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{fe}\right)  $ are given by
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  f_{k}\right)  _{p},\,1\leq p\leq r,\,1\leq k\leq r.
\end{equation}


\item In general
\begin{equation}
\left[  f_{k}e_{k}^{d}\right]  \leftrightarrow\left\vert f_{k}\right\rangle
\left\langle e_{k}\right\vert
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\left[  \mathbf{fe}^{d}\right]  \leftrightarrow\left\vert \mathbf{f}%
\right\rangle \left\langle \mathbf{e}\right\vert .
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{remark}
General bases for $\mathcal{V}$ are denoted by $\left\{  \left.
e_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq n\right\}  $ or $\left\{  \left.  v_{j}%
\right\vert 1\leq j\leq n\right\}  ,$ while general bases for $\mathcal{W}$
are denoted by $\left\{  \left.  f_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq m\right\}  $ or
$\left\{  \left.  w_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq m\right\}  .$
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
For any bases $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ and $\left(  \mathbf{f}\right)  $

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{C}_{e}\left(  U\right)  _{k}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W}%
,\mathcal{W}\right)  ,\quad1\leq k\leq m
\end{equation}
and

\item
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{C}_{f}\left(  Z\right)  _{k}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}%
,\mathcal{V}\right)  ,\quad1\leq k\leq n,
\end{equation}
where we have "padded" the operators $U$ and $Z$ to the dimensions of
$\mathcal{V}$ and $\mathcal{W}$ with vectors corresponding to \emph{zero
singular values}.
\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}

\subparagraph{Spectral Theorem}

Let $\mathcal{V}$ be a Banach space of $\dim\left\{  \mathcal{V}\right\}  =r$
then one has the spectral theorem:

\begin{theorem}
The \emph{spectral expansion} of compact normal operators $A\in\mathcal{L}%
\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  $ is given by
\begin{equation}
A=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}\left[  a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}\left[  a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  _{jj},\quad
z_{j}\in\operatorname*{Spec}\left\{  A\right\}  \subseteq\mathbb{C},
\end{equation}
where $\left\{  \left.  \left[  a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]  \right\vert 1\leq j\leq
r\right\}  $ are projection operators onto the eigen subspaces generated by
the eigenvectors $\left\{  \left.  a_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\}  $
of $A.$
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
One has the resolution of the identity generated by the basis $\left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  $%
\begin{equation}
I_{r}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[  e_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  =\left[
\mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]  =\mathbf{e}\diamond\mathbf{e}^{dt}=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1} & \cdots & e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}%
\begin{equation}
A=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  _{j}\left[  a_{j}%
a_{j}^{d}\right]  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[  e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[
T_{ae}\left(  e_{j}\right)  T_{a^{d}e^{d}}\left(  e_{k}^{d}\right)  \right]
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[  \left[
\mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \left(  e_{j}\right)  \left[  \mathbf{a}^{d}%
\mathbf{e}\right]  \left(  e_{k}^{d}\right)  \right]  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
A\right)  _{jk}%
\end{equation}

\end{corollary}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Using the resolution of the identity $\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]  $
the operator $A\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  $ can be expanded as
\begin{align}
A  &  =\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]  A\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]
=\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  A\mathbf{e}\right)
\right)  \left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  ^{t}=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1} & \cdots & e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  A\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1} & \cdots & e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1} & \cdots & e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
Ae_{1} & \cdots & Ae_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1} & \cdots & e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1}^{d}\left(  Ae_{1}\right)  & \cdots & e_{1}^{d}\left(  Ae_{r}\right) \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
e_{1}^{d}\left(  Ae_{r}\right)  & \cdots & e_{r}^{d}\left(  Ae_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  ,
\end{align}


\item Using the resolution of the identity $\left[  \mathbf{aa}^{d}\right]  $
one obtains the spectral expansion of $A$
\begin{align}
A  &  =\left[  \mathbf{aa}^{d}\right]  A\left[  \mathbf{aa}^{d}\right]
=\left(  \mathbf{a}\right)  \left(  \mathbf{a}^{d}\left(  A\mathbf{a}\right)
\right)  \left(  \mathbf{a}^{d}\right)  ^{t}=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
a_{1} & \cdots & a_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
a_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
a_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  A\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
a_{1} & \cdots & a_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
a_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
a_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
a_{1} & \cdots & a_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
a_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
a_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
Aa_{1} & \cdots & Aa_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
a_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
a_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cccc}%
a_{1} & \cdots & \cdots & a_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cccc}%
z_{1} & 0 & \cdots & 0\\
0 & \ddots & \ddots & \vdots\\
\vdots & \ddots & \ddots & 0\\
0 & \cdots & 0 & z_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
a_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
\vdots\\
a_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  ,
\end{align}

\end{enumerate}

\begin{remark}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Using the spectral expansion and the resolution of the identities
$\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]  $ and $\left[  \mathbf{aa}^{d}\right]  $ $A$
can also be expanded as%
\begin{align}
A  &  =\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]  A\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]
=\left[  \mathbf{ee}\right]  \left[  \mathbf{aa}^{d}\right]  A\left[
\mathbf{aa}^{d}\right]  \left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1} & \cdots & e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  A\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1} & \cdots & e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1} & \cdots & e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
a_{1} & \cdots & a_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
z_{1} & \cdots & 0\\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
0 & \cdots & z_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
a_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
a_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1} & \cdots & e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1} & \cdots & e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1}^{d}\left(  a_{1}\right)  & \cdots & e_{1}^{d}\left(  a_{r}\right) \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}\left(  a_{1}\right)  & \cdots & e_{r}^{d}\left(  a_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
z_{1} & \cdots & 0\\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
0 & \cdots & z_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
a_{1}^{d}\left(  e_{1}\right)  & \cdots & a_{1}^{d}\left(  e_{r}\right) \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
a_{r}^{d}\left(  e_{1}\right)  & \cdots & a_{r}^{d}\left(  e_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1} & \cdots & e_{r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{a}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}%
^{dt}\right]  \right)  _{k}z_{k}\sum_{1\leq l\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{a}\left(
\left[  \mathbf{ea}^{dt}\right]  \right)  _{l}\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
e_{1}^{d}\\
\vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \sum_{1\leq k,l\leq r}\left[  a_{k}e_{k}%
^{d}\right]  z_{k}\left[  e_{l}a_{l}^{d}\right]  \right)  .
\end{align}


\item
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{C}_{e}\left(  U\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[  e_{j}%
e_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \mathfrak{C}_{e}\left(  U\right)
_{k}\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[  e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  U\right)  _{jk}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W}%
\right)  ,\quad1\leq k\leq r
\end{equation}


\begin{enumerate}
\item Columns of $T_{ae}$ in $\left(  \mathbf{a}\right)  $-basis are the
eigenvectors $\left\{  a_{k}\right\}  $%
\begin{equation}
T_{ae}=\sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{a}\left(  T_{ae}\right)  _{k}%
=\sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{a}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[  a_{j}a_{k}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  T_{ae}\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[
a_{j}a_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{jk}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)
\end{equation}


\item Columns of $T_{ae}$ in $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $-basis are the
eigenvectors $\left\{  a_{k}\right\}  $%
\begin{equation}
T_{ae}=\sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{a}\left(  T_{ae}\right)  _{k}%
=\sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{a}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[  e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ae}\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[
e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{jk}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{align}
\mathfrak{C}_{a}\left(  T_{ae}\right)  _{k}  &  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[
e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[  e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  a_{k}\right)  _{j}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[
e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]  e_{j}^{d}\left(  a_{k}\right) \\
&  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left\vert e_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle
e_{k}\right\vert \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  a_{k}\right)  _{j}=\left\vert
a_{k}\right\rangle \left\langle e_{k}\right\vert =\left[  a_{k}e_{k}%
^{d}\right]
\end{align}

\end{enumerate}

\item
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \right)   &
=\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ae}\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
e_{1}^{d}\left(  a_{1}\right)  & \cdots & e_{1}^{d}\left(  a_{r}\right) \\
\vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
e_{r}^{d}\left(  a_{1}\right)  & \cdots & e_{r}^{d}\left(  a_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right) \\
&  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
a_{1}^{d}\left(  v_{1}\right) \\
\vdots\\
a_{r}^{d}\left(  v_{1}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  &
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\cdots\\
\ddots\\
\cdots
\end{array}
& \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
a_{1}^{d}\left(  v_{r}\right) \\
\vdots\\
a_{r}^{d}\left(  v_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right)
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{ccc}%
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  a_{1}\right)  & \cdots & \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
a_{r}\right)
\end{array}
\right)  .
\end{align}


\item
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ae}\right)   &  =\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[
\mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \right)  =\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}%
^{d}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \right)  =\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(
\mathbf{a}\right) \\
T_{ae}  &  =\left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[
a_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  .
\end{align}

\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}
For any basis $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ of $\mathcal{V}$ the
representation $\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  $ of $A$ is given by
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  =\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  A\mathbf{e}\right)
=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  a_{j}a_{j}%
^{d}\right]  \right)  =\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}%
z_{j}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]  \right)
\mathbf{e}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \left[
a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]  \mathbf{e}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{gather}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{kl}=e_{k}^{d}\left(  Ae_{l}\right)
=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  a_{j}a_{j}%
^{d}\right]  \right)  _{kl}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}e_{k}^{d}\left(  \left[
a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]  e_{l}\right) \\
1\leq k,l\leq r,
\end{gather}
in particular
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}\mathbf{a}%
^{d}\left(  \left[  a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]  \mathbf{a}\right)  =\mathbf{a}%
^{d}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}\left[  a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]
\mathbf{a}\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{gather}
\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  _{kl}=a_{k}^{d}\left(  Aa_{l}\right)
=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  \left[  a_{j}a_{j}%
^{d}\right]  \right)  _{kl}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}a_{k}^{d}\left(  \left[
a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]  a_{l}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}a_{k}%
^{d}\left(  a_{j}\right)  a_{j}^{d}\left(  a_{l}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}z_{j}\delta_{jk}\delta_{jl}=z_{k}\delta_{kl}\\
1\leq k,l\leq r.
\end{gather}

\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
$A$ can be expanded as
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  =\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  A\mathbf{e}\right)
=\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{aa}^{d}\right]  A\left[  \mathbf{aa}%
^{d}\right]  \mathbf{e}\right)  =\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \mathbf{a}\right)
\mathbf{a}^{d}\left(  A\mathbf{a}\right)  \mathbf{a}^{d}\left(  \mathbf{e}%
\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \right)
\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ea}%
^{d}\right]  \right)
\end{equation}
hence
\begin{equation}
A=\left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  A\left[  \mathbf{ea}^{d}\right]  =\left(
\mathbf{a}\right)  \mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  A\mathbf{e}\right)  \left(
\mathbf{a}^{d}\right)  ^{t}=\left(  \mathbf{a}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
A\right)  \left(  \mathbf{a}^{d}\right)  ^{t}.
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
$A$ can be expanded as%
\begin{equation}
A=\left[  \mathbf{ea}^{d}\right]  A\left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  =\left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  \mathbf{a}^{d}\left(  A\mathbf{a}\right)  \left(
\mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  ^{t}.=\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(
A\right)  \left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  ^{t}.
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The change of basis $T_{ae}$ of $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ to $\left(
\mathbf{a}\right)  $ is
\begin{equation}
T_{ae}=\left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]
\end{equation}
and the change of basis $T_{ea}=$ $T_{ae}^{-1}$ of $\left(  \mathbf{a}\right)
$ to $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ is
\begin{equation}
T_{ea}=\left[  \mathbf{ea}^{d}\right]  .
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The representation matrix $\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ae}\right)  $ of the
change of basis operator$T_{ae}$ of $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ to $\left(
\mathbf{a}\right)  $ is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ae}\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[
\mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \right)  =\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}%
^{d}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \right)  =\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(
\mathbf{a}\right)
\end{equation}
and the change of basis $\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ea}\right)  =$
$\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ae}^{-1}\right)  $ of $\left(  \mathbf{a}\right)  $
to $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $ is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ea}\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[
\mathbf{ea}^{d}\right]  \right)  =\mathbf{e}^{d}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ea}%
^{d}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \right)  =\mathbf{a}^{d}\left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{definition}
Column Operators of $T_{ea}$
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{C}_{e}\left(  T_{ea}\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left[
e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ea}\right)  _{jk}%
\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  ,\quad1\leq k\leq r
\end{equation}
Columns of $T_{ea}$ in $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $-basis are the vectors
$\left\{  e_{k}\right\}  $%
\begin{equation}
T_{ea}=\sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{e}\left(  T_{ea}\right)  _{k}%
=\sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ea}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[  e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ea}\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[
e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ea}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{jk}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  .
\end{equation}
Columns of $T_{ea}$ in $\left(  \mathbf{a}\right)  $-basis are the
eigenvectors $\left\{  e_{k}\right\}  $%
\begin{equation}
T_{ea}=\sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{e}\left(  T_{ea}\right)  _{k}%
=\sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ea}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[  a_{j}a_{k}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  T_{ea}\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[
a_{j}a_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ea}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{jk}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  .
\end{equation}
Columns of $T_{ae}$ in $\left(  \mathbf{a}\right)  $-basis are the
eigenvectors $\left\{  a_{k}\right\}  $%
\begin{equation}
T_{ae}=\sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{a}\left(  T_{ae}\right)  _{k}%
=\sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{a}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[  a_{j}a_{k}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  T_{ae}\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[
a_{j}a_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{jk}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  .
\end{equation}
Columns of $T_{ae}$ in $\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  $-basis are the
eigenvectors $\left\{  a_{k}\right\}  $%
\begin{equation}
T_{ae}=\sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{a}\left(  T_{ae}\right)  _{k}%
=\sum_{1\leq k\leq r}\mathfrak{C}_{a}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{k}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[  e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ae}\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[
e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]
\right)  _{jk}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
Recall the dual map $T^{d}$ of $T$ is defined by
\begin{equation}
\left\langle T^{d}v^{d},w\right\rangle =\left\langle v^{d},Tw\right\rangle .
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The change of basis $T_{ae}^{d}$ of $\left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  $ to
$\left(  \mathbf{a}^{d}\right)  $ is
\begin{equation}
T_{ae}^{d}=\left[  \mathbf{a}^{d}\mathbf{e}\right]
\end{equation}
and the change of basis $T_{ea}^{d}=$ $T_{ae}^{-d}$ of $\left(  \mathbf{a}%
^{d}\right)  $ to $\left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  $ is
\begin{equation}
T_{ea}^{d}=\left[  \mathbf{e}^{d}\mathbf{a}\right]  .
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
A compact normal operator $A$ can be expanded as
\begin{equation}
A=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}\left[  a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]  =\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\left[  e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{jk}%
=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[  a_{j}a_{k}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(
A\right)  _{jk},\quad z\in\mathbb{C}%
\end{equation}
hence
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  _{jk}=z_{j}\delta_{jk},\quad1\leq j,k\leq r.
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
A compact normal $A$ can be expanded as

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
A=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}\left[  a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]  =\left(
\mathbf{a}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  \left(  \mathbf{a}%
^{d}\right)  ^{t}=\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)
\left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  ^{t}%
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\left(  \mathbf{a}\right)  =T_{ea}\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  =\left[
\mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  =\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \right)  \equiv\left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  T_{ea}\right)
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
a_{j}=\left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \left(  e_{j}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq k\leq
r}\left[  a_{k}e_{k}^{d}\right]  \left(  e_{j}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq k\leq
r}e_{k}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \right)  _{jk}%
\end{equation}


\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
A=\left(  \mathbf{a}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  \left(
\mathbf{a}^{d}\right)  ^{t}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}a_{j}\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(
A\right)  _{jk}a_{k}^{d}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[  a_{j}a_{k}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  _{jk}=\left[  \mathbf{aa}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
A=\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  \left(
\mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  ^{t}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}e_{j}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
A\right)  _{jk}e_{k}^{d}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\left[  e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  _{jk}=\left[  \mathbf{ee}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{align}
A  &  =\left(  \mathbf{a}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  \left(
\mathbf{a}^{d}\right)  ^{t}=\left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  \left(  \left[
\mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  ^{d}\left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  \right)
^{t}=\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)
\right)  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  \left(  \left[  \mathbf{a}%
^{d}\mathbf{e}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  \right)  ^{t}=\\
&  =\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \left(  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[
\mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \right)  \right)  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)
\left(  \mathcal{R}_{e^{d}}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{a}^{d}\mathbf{e}\right]
\right)  \right)  ^{t}\left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  ^{t}%
\end{align}


\item
\begin{align}
A  &  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}\left[  a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]  =\left(
\mathbf{a}\right)  \boldsymbol{z}\left(  \mathbf{a}^{d}\right)  ^{t}=\left(
\left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \right)
\boldsymbol{z}\left(  \left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  \right)  ^{d}\right)  ^{t}\\
&  =\left(  \left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[
\mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \right)  \right)  \boldsymbol{z}\left(  \left(
\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}%
^{d}\right]  \right)  \right)  ^{d}\right)  ^{t}=\left(  \left(
\mathbf{e}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]
\right)  \right)  \boldsymbol{z}\left(  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[
\mathbf{ae}^{d}\right]  \right)  \right)  ^{dt}\left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)
^{t}\\
&  =\left(  \mathbf{e}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{ae}%
^{d}\right]  \right)  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  \left(  \mathcal{R}%
_{e^{d}}\left(  \left[  \mathbf{a}^{d}\mathbf{e}\right]  \right)  \right)
^{t}\left(  \mathbf{e}^{d}\right)  ^{t}\equiv\left\vert \overset{}{\mathbf{e}%
}\right\rangle \left(  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \overset{}{\left\vert
\mathbf{a}\right\rangle \left\langle \mathbf{e}\right\vert }\right)
\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  \overset{}{A}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
\overset{}{\left\vert \mathbf{a}\right\rangle \left\langle \mathbf{e}%
\right\vert }\right)  ^{\dagger}\right)  \left\langle \overset{}{\mathbf{e}%
}\right\vert \\
&  =\left\vert \overset{}{\mathbf{e}}\right\rangle \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
\overset{}{A}\right)  \left\langle \overset{}{\mathbf{e}}\right\vert
=\left\vert \overset{}{\mathbf{a}}\right\rangle \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(
\overset{}{A}\right)  \left\langle \overset{}{\mathbf{a}}\right\vert .
\end{align}


\item The eigenvectors $\left\{  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  a_{j}\right)
\right\}  $ form the columns of unitary transformation $\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
\left\vert \mathbf{a}\right\rangle \left\langle \mathbf{e}\right\vert \right)
$
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left\vert \mathbf{a}\right\rangle \left\langle
\mathbf{e}\right\vert \right)  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(
\overset{}{\left\vert a_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \mathbf{e}\right\vert
}\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left\vert
a_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \mathbf{e}\right\vert \right)  =\left(
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
a_{11}\\
\vdots\\
a_{1r}%
\end{array}
\right)  \cdots\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
a_{r1}\\
\vdots\\
a_{r1}%
\end{array}
\right)  \right)
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  A\right)  =\mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left\vert
\mathbf{a}\right\rangle \left\langle \mathbf{e}\right\vert \right)
\mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  \mathcal{R}_{e}\left(  \left\vert
\mathbf{a}\right\rangle \left\langle \mathbf{e}\right\vert \right)  ^{\dagger}%
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{align}
A  &  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}\left[  a_{j}a_{j}^{d}\right]  =\sum_{1\leq
k.l\leq r}\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}\left[  \left[  e_{k}e_{k}^{d}\right]
\left(  a_{j}\right)  \left(  \left[  e_{l}e_{l}^{d}\right]  \left(
a_{j}\right)  \right)  ^{d}\right]  =\sum_{1\leq k.l\leq r}\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}z_{j}\left[  \left[  e_{k}e_{k}^{d}\left(  a_{j}\right)  \right]  \left(
\left[  e_{l}e_{l}^{d}\left(  a_{j}\right)  \right]  \right)  ^{d}\right] \\
&  =\sum_{1\leq k.l\leq r}\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}z_{j}e_{k}^{d}\left(
a_{j}\right)  e_{l}^{d}\left(  a_{j}\right)  \left[  e_{k}e_{l}^{d}\right]
=\sum_{1\leq k.l\leq r}\left[  e_{k}e_{l}^{d}\right]  \sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}e_{l}^{d}\left(  a_{j}\right)  z_{j}e_{k}^{d}\left(  a_{j}\right)
=\sum_{1\leq k.l\leq r}\left[  e_{k}e_{l}^{d}\right]  \sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}e_{l}^{d}\left(  a_{j}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{a}\left(  A\right)  e_{k}%
^{d}\left(  a_{j}\right)
\end{align}

\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}

\subparagraph{Singular Valued Decomposition Theorem}

\begin{theorem}
\emph{Singular Valued Decomposition (SVD)}: If $u_{k}$ and $z_{k}$ for $1\leq
k\leq\min(m,n)$ are \emph{left-singular} and \emph{right-singular} vectors for
the singular value $\U{3c3} _{k}$ of $M,$
\begin{equation}
M:\mathcal{V\rightarrow W}%
\end{equation}
then $M$ can be expanded as
\begin{equation}
M=\sum_{1\leq j\leq\min(m,n)}\U{3c3} _{j}\left[  u_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]
\end{equation}
and this expansion \emph{is a} \emph{SVD of }$M.$ The expansion is unique up
to transformations mixing degenerate singular vectors.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}%
\begin{equation}
Mz_{k}=\U{3c3} _{k}u_{k},\quad1\leq k\leq\min(m,n)
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
M^{\dagger}u_{k}=\U{3c3} _{k}z_{k},\quad1\leq k\leq\min(m,n).
\end{equation}

\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
In any singular value decomposition%
\begin{equation}
M=U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger}%
\end{equation}
one has the expansions
\begin{align*}
M  &  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j\leq n\\1\leq k\leq m}}\left[  w_{j}v_{k}%
^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{vw}\left(  M\right)  _{jk}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1},j_{2}\leq n\\1\leq k_{3}\leq m}}\left[
w_{j_{1}}w_{j_{2}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{j_{1}j_{2}%
}\left[  w_{j_{2}}v_{j_{2}}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{wv}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)
_{j_{2}j_{2}}\left[  v_{j_{2}}v_{k_{3}}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(
Z^{\dagger}\right)  _{j_{2}k_{3}}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1},j_{2}\leq n\\1\leq k_{3}\leq m}}\left[
w_{j_{1}}w_{j_{2}}^{d}\right]  \left[  w_{j_{2}}v_{j_{2}}\right]  \left[
v_{j_{2}}v_{k_{3}}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{j_{1}j_{2}%
}\mathcal{R}_{wv}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)  _{j_{2}j_{2}}\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(
Z^{\dagger}\right)  _{j_{2}k_{3}}.
\end{align*}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
$M$ has a \emph{SVD} given by
\end{remark}

%

\begin{equation}
M=U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger},
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\min(m,n)}\U{3c3} _{j}\left[  u_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]
.
\end{equation}


\begin{remark}%
\begin{equation}
Z^{\dagger}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V},\mathcal{V}\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} \in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
U\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W},\mathcal{W}\right)
\end{equation}

\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
If
\begin{equation}
Mz_{k}=\U{3c3} _{k}u_{k}^{d},\quad1\leq k\leq\min(m,n)
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
M^{d}u_{k}^{d}=\U{3c3} _{k}z_{k},\quad1\leq k\leq\min(m,n).
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
M=U\U{3a3} Z^{d}=\sum_{1\leq k\leq\min(m,n)}\left[  u_{k}z_{k}\right]  \U{3c3}
_{k}%
\end{equation}
and
\begin{align}
M  &  =U\U{3a3} Z^{d}\\
\quad\quad &  \parallel
\end{align}%
\begin{gather*}
\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1},j_{2}\leq n\\1\leq k_{3}\leq m}}\left[  u_{j_{1}%
}u_{j_{2}}^{d}\right]  \left[  u_{j_{3}}z_{j_{3}}\right]  \left[  z_{k_{2}%
}^{d}z_{k_{3}}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{u}\left(  U\right)  _{j_{1}j_{2}%
}\mathcal{R}_{uz^{d}}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)  _{j_{2}j_{2}}\mathcal{R}_{z^{d}%
}\left(  Z^{d}\right)  _{j_{2}k_{3}}\\
\parallel\\
\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{3}\leq m}}\left[  u_{j_{1}%
}z_{k_{3}}\right]  \sum_{1\leq j_{2}\leq\min\left(  n,m\right)  }%
\mathcal{R}_{u}\left(  U\right)  _{j_{1}j_{2}}\U{3c3} _{j_{2}}\mathcal{R}%
_{z^{d}}\left(  Z^{d}\right)  _{j_{2}k_{3}}.
\end{gather*}

\end{remark}

\begin{enumerate}
\item In any singular value decomposition%
\begin{equation}
M=U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger}%
\end{equation}
the "diagonal entries" of $\U{3a3} $ are equal to the singular values of $M$.

\item $\U{3a3} $ is the \emph{singular expansion} of $M$ in the singular
vector basis.

\item $U$ \emph{diagonalises} $MM^{\dagger}$
\begin{equation}
MM^{\dagger}=U\U{3a3} V^{\dagger}V\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}U^{\dagger}=U\U{3a3}
\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}U^{\dagger}%
\end{equation}


\item $\U{3a3} \U{3a3} ^{\dagger}$ is the \emph{spectral expansion} of
$MM^{\dagger}$

\item $Z$ diagonalises $M^{\dagger}M$%
\begin{equation}
M^{\dagger}M=Z\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}U^{\dagger}U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger}=Z\U{3a3}
^{\dagger}\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger}%
\end{equation}


\item $\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}\U{3a3} $ is the \emph{spectral expansion} of
$M^{\dagger}M$

\item $Z$ is unitary
\begin{equation}
Z,Z^{\dagger}:\mathcal{V}\rightarrow\mathcal{V},\quad Z\in\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{V}\right)  \cong\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathbb{C}^{m}\right)
\end{equation}
i.e.
\begin{equation}
Z^{\dagger}Z=I_{m}.
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
Z:\mathcal{V}\rightarrow\mathcal{W},\quad\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}%
,\mathcal{W}\right)  \cong\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathbb{C}^{m},\mathbb{C}%
^{n}\right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}

\begin{remark}
\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} \U{3a3} ^{\dagger}:\mathcal{W\rightarrow W}%
\end{equation}
i.e.%
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} \U{3a3} ^{\dagger}\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W}\right)
\cong\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathbb{C}^{n}\right)  .
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}\U{3a3} :\mathcal{V\rightarrow V}%
\end{equation}
i.e.%
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}\U{3a3} \in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}\right)
\cong\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathbb{C}^{m}\right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}

\item $U$ is unitary%
\begin{equation}
U,U^{\dagger}:\mathcal{W\rightarrow W},\quad U\in\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathcal{W}\right)  \cong\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathbb{C}^{n}\right)
\end{equation}
i.e.
\begin{equation}
U^{\dagger}U=I_{n}.
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}\left[
u_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma
_{j}E_{u_{j}z_{j}^{d}},\quad E_{u_{j}z_{j}^{d}}=\left[  u_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]
\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}\right)  \cong\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathbb{C}^{m},\mathbb{C}^{n}\right)  ,\quad\sigma_{j}\geq0,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
E_{u_{j}z_{j}^{d}}:\mathcal{V}\rightarrow\mathcal{W},\quad\in\mathcal{L}%
\left(  \mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}\right)  \cong\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathbb{C}%
^{m},\mathbb{C}^{n}\right)  .
\end{equation}
Note that it is possible $\sigma_{j}=0$ for some $j\in\mathbb{N}^{\min\left(
m,n\right)  }.$

\item The general SVD of $M$ is given by
\begin{equation}
M=U\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}\left[
w_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]  \right)  Z^{\dagger}%
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\operatorname*{Range}\left(  M\right)  =\mathbb{C}\text{-}%
\operatorname*{linspan}\left\{  \left.  w_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
n\right\}
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} z_{j}=\sigma_{j}u_{j},\quad1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} ^{d}u_{j}^{d}=\sigma_{j}z_{j}^{d}%
\end{equation}


\item In general $T\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}\right)  $
\begin{align}
T  &  :\mathcal{V}\rightarrow\mathcal{W}\\
T  &  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j\leq n\\1\leq k\leq m}}\left[  w_{j}v_{k}%
^{d}\right]  T_{jk}\\
T\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)   &  =\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  T\right) \\
\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  T\right)   &  \in\mathcal{M}\left(  m,n,\mathbb{C}%
\right)  .
\end{align}


\item In general%
\begin{align}
v  &  =\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  v\right)
=\sum_{1\leq j\leq m}v_{j}\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  v\right)  _{j}\\
T\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)   &  =\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)
\mathcal{R}_{wv}\left(  T\right)  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j\leq n\\1\leq k\leq
m}}w_{k}\mathcal{R}_{wv}\left(  T\right)  _{kj}\\
Tv  &  =T\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  v\right)
=\left(  \mathbf{v}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{wv}\left(  T\right)  \mathcal{R}%
_{v}\left(  v\right)  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j\leq n\\1\leq k\leq m}%
}w_{k}\mathcal{R}_{wv}\left(  T\right)  _{kj}\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  v\right)
_{j}.
\end{align}


\item
\begin{equation}
\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}u_{j}v_{j}%
^{d}\right)  \left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)  =\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq
\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}u_{j}v_{j}^{d}\right)  \left(  v_{1}%
,\cdots,v_{r}\right)  =\left(  \sigma_{1}u_{1},\cdots,\sigma_{r}u_{r}\right)
=\left(  v_{1},\cdots,v_{r}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\left(  \boldsymbol{w}\right)  =\U{3a3} \left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)
=\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)  \mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)
=\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}u_{j}v_{j}%
^{d}\right)  \left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)  =\left(  \boldsymbol{v}\right)
\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  \sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma
_{j}u_{j}v_{j}^{d}\right)
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)  =\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  \sum_{1\leq
j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}u_{j}v_{j}^{d}\right)  =\mathcal{M}%
_{uv}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}
Adjoint: The adjoint operation is \emph{defined} as an anti linear operator
"$\dagger"\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \overset{}{\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}%
,\mathcal{W}\right)  ,\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W},\mathcal{V}\right)
}\right)  $ that has the effect
\begin{equation}
\left(  E_{w_{j}v_{j}^{d}}\right)  ^{\dagger}=E_{v_{j}w_{j}^{d}}=\left[
v_{j}w_{j}^{d}\right]  ,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
E_{v_{j}w_{j}^{d}}:\mathcal{W}\rightarrow\mathcal{V},\quad\in\mathcal{L}%
\left(  \mathcal{W},\mathcal{V}\right)  \cong\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathbb{C}%
^{n},\mathbb{C}^{m}\right)  .
\end{equation}

\end{definition}

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{align}
\U{3a3}  &  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}u_{j}%
z_{j}^{d}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}E_{u_{j}%
z_{j}^{d}},\quad E_{u_{j}z_{j}^{d}}=\left[  u_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]
\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}\right)  \cong\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathbb{C}^{m},\mathbb{C}^{n}\right) \\
\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}  &  =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma
_{j}z_{j}u_{j}^{d}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma
_{j}E_{z_{j}u_{j}^{d}},\quad E_{z_{j}u_{j}^{d}}=\left[  z_{j}u_{j}^{d}\right]
\in\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W},\mathcal{V}\right)  \cong\mathcal{L}\left(
\mathbb{C}^{n},\mathbb{C}^{m}\right)  .
\end{align}


\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} z_{j}=\sigma_{j}u_{j}%
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}u_{j}=\sigma_{j}z_{j}%
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
u_{j}^{d}\U{3a3} =\sigma_{j}z_{j}^{d}%
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
z_{j}^{d}\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}=\sigma_{j}u_{j}^{d}.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

\begin{remark}
The \emph{Structure Coefficients} of $\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V}%
,\mathcal{W}\right)  $ and $\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{W},\mathcal{V}\right)
:$ Products of the elementary operators $\left[  f_{l}e_{m}^{d}\right]  $ and
their adjoints are given by

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\left[  f_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]  ^{\dagger}\left[  f_{l}e_{m}^{d}\right]
=\left[  e_{k}f_{j}^{d}\right]  \left[  f_{l}e_{m}^{d}\right]  =\left[
e_{k}e_{m}^{d}\right]  \delta_{jl}%
\end{equation}
and

\item
\begin{equation}
\left[  f_{l}e_{m}^{d}\right]  \left[  f_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]  ^{\dagger
}=\left[  f_{l}e_{m}^{d}\right]  \left[  e_{k}f_{j}^{d}\right]  =\left[
f_{l}f_{j}^{d}\right]  \delta_{km}.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}

\item $\left[  f_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  $ are not normal operators as in general
as they do not commute with their adjoints i.e.

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
\left[  f_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  ^{\dagger}\left[  f_{k}e_{k}^{d}\right]
=\left[  e_{j}f_{j}^{d}\right]  \left[  f_{k}e_{k}^{d}\right]  =\left[
e_{j}e_{k}^{d}\right]  f_{j}^{d}\left(  f_{k}\right)  =\left[  e_{j}e_{j}%
^{d}\right]  \delta_{jk}.
\end{equation}
and

\item
\begin{equation}
\left[  f_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  \left[  f_{k}e_{k}^{d}\right]  ^{\dagger
}=\left[  f_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  \left[  e_{k}f_{k}^{d}\right]  =\left[
f_{j}f_{k}^{d}\right]  e_{j}^{d}\left(  e_{k}\right)  =\left[  f_{j}f_{j}%
^{d}\right]  \delta_{jk}.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}

\item $\left[  f_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  $ are partial isometries as $\left[
e_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  $ and $\left[  f_{j}f_{j}^{d}\right]  $ are projectors
i.e.
\begin{equation}
\left[  e_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]  ^{2}=\left[  e_{j}e_{j}^{d}\right]
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\left[  f_{j}f_{j}^{d}\right]  ^{2}=\left[  f_{j}f_{j}^{d}\right]  .
\end{equation}


\begin{enumerate}
\item Singular expansion $\U{3a3} $ of $M$%
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}\left[
u_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]  ,\quad\sigma_{j}>0.
\end{equation}


\item Singular expansion $\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}$ of $M^{\dagger}$%
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma
_{j}\left[  z_{j}u_{j}^{d}\right]  ,\quad\sigma_{j}>0.
\end{equation}


\item Spectral expansion $\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}\U{3a3} $ of $M^{\dagger}M$%
\begin{align}
\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}\U{3a3}  &  =\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)
}\sigma_{j}\sigma_{k}\left[  u_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]  ^{\dagger}\left[
u_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]  =\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma
_{j}\sigma_{k}\left[  z_{j}u_{j}^{d}\right]  \left[  u_{k}z_{k}^{d}\right] \\
&  =\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}\sigma_{k}\left[
z_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]  u_{j}^{d}\left(  u_{k}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq
\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}\sigma_{k}\left[  z_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]
\delta_{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}^{2}\left[
z_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]  .
\end{align}


\item Spectral expansion $\U{3a3} \U{3a3} ^{\dagger}$ of $MM^{\dagger}$%
\begin{align}
\U{3a3} \U{3a3} ^{\dagger}  &  =\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)
}\sigma_{j}\sigma_{k}\left[  u_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]  \left[  u_{k}z_{k}%
^{d}\right]  ^{\dagger}=\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma
_{j}\sigma_{k}\left[  u_{j}z_{j}^{d}\right]  \left[  z_{k}u_{k}^{d}\right] \\
&  =\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}^{2}\left[
u_{j}u_{k}^{d}\right]  z_{j}^{d}\left(  z_{k}\right)  =\sum_{1\leq j,k\leq
\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}\sigma_{k}\left[  u_{j}u_{k}^{d}\right]
\delta_{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}^{2}\left[
u_{j}u_{j}^{d}\right]  .
\end{align}

\end{enumerate}

\item Expansion of $M$%
\begin{align}
M  &  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j,\leq n\\1\leq k\leq m}}\left[  z_{j}u_{k}%
^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{zu}\left(  M\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{1\leq j_{1}%
,j_{2}\leq n}\left[  u_{j_{1}}u_{j_{2}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{u}\left(
U\right)  _{j_{1}j_{2}}\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{3}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1}\leq
m}}\left[  u_{j_{3}}z_{k_{1}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  \U{3a3}
\right)  _{j_{3}k_{1}}\sum_{1\leq k_{2},k_{3}\leq m}\left[  z_{k_{2}}z_{k_{3}%
}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{z}\left(  V^{\dagger}\right)  _{k_{2}k_{3}}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1},j_{2},j_{3}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1},k_{2},k_{3}\leq
m}}\left[  u_{j_{1}}u_{j_{2}}^{d}\right]  \left[  u_{j_{3}}z_{k_{1}}%
^{d}\right]  \left[  z_{k_{2}}z_{k_{3}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{u}\left(
U\right)  _{j_{1}j_{2}}\mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)  _{j_{3}k_{1}%
}\mathcal{R}_{z}\left(  z^{\dagger}\right)  _{k_{2}k_{3}}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1},j_{2}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1},k_{3}\leq m}}\left[
u_{j_{1}}z_{k_{3}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{u}\left(  U\right)  _{j_{1}j_{2}%
}\mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)  _{j_{2}k_{1}}\mathcal{R}_{z}\left(
z^{\dagger}\right)  _{k_{1}k_{3}}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1},j_{2}\leq n\\1\leq j_{2}\leq\min\left(
n,m\right)  \\1\leq k_{3}\leq m}}\left[  u_{j_{1}}z_{k_{3}}^{d}\right]
\mathcal{R}_{u}\left(  U\right)  _{j_{1}j_{2}}\mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  \U{3a3}
\right)  _{j_{2}j_{2}}\mathcal{R}_{z}\left(  z^{\dagger}\right)  _{j_{2}k_{3}%
}=\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1},\leq n\\1\leq k_{3}\leq m}}\left[  z_{j_{1}%
}u_{k_{3}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{zu}\left(  M\right)  _{j_{1}k_{3}}.
\end{align}


\item The columns of $Z$ (right-singular vectors) are eigenvectors of
$M^{\dagger}M$%
\begin{align}
M^{\dagger}M  &  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1}\leq
m}}\left[  z_{j_{1}}u_{k_{1}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  M\right)
_{j_{1}k_{1}}^{\dagger}\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{2}\leq n\\1\leq k_{2}\leq
m}}\left[  u_{j_{2}}z_{k_{2}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  M\right)
_{j_{2}k_{2}}=\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1}\leq m}%
}\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{2}\leq n\\1\leq k_{2}\leq m}}\left[  z_{j_{1}%
}u_{k_{1}}^{d}\right]  \left[  u_{j_{2}}z_{k_{2}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}%
_{uz}\left(  M\right)  _{j_{1}k_{1}}^{\dagger}\mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(
M\right)  _{j_{2}k_{2}}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1}\leq m\\1\leq k_{2}\leq
m}}\left[  z_{j_{1}}z_{k_{2}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  M\right)
_{j_{1}k_{1}}^{\dagger}\mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  M\right)  _{k_{1}k_{2}}%
=\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{2}\leq m}}\left[  z_{j_{1}%
}z_{k_{2}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  M^{\dagger}M\right)
_{j_{1}k_{2}}%
\end{align}
and the columns of $U$ (left-singular vectors) are eigenvectors of
$MM^{\dagger}$%
\begin{align}
MM^{\dagger}  &  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{2}\leq n\\1\leq k_{2}\leq
m}}\left[  u_{j_{2}}z_{k_{2}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  M\right)
_{j_{2}k_{2}}\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1}\leq m}}\left[
z_{j_{1}}u_{k_{1}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  M\right)  _{j_{1}k_{1}%
}^{\dagger}=\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1}\leq m}%
}\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{2}\leq n\\1\leq k_{2}\leq m}}\left[  u_{j_{2}%
}z_{k_{2}}^{d}\right]  \left[  z_{j_{1}}u_{k_{1}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}%
_{uz}\left(  M\right)  _{j_{2}k_{2}}\mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  M\right)
_{j_{1}k_{1}}^{\dagger}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1}\leq m\\1\leq k_{2}\leq
m}}\left[  u_{j_{1}}u_{k_{2}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  M\right)
_{j_{1}k_{1}}^{\dagger}\mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  M\right)  _{k_{1}k_{2}}%
=\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{2}\leq m}}\left[  u_{j_{1}%
}u_{k_{2}}^{d}\right]  \mathcal{R}_{uz}\left(  MM^{\dagger}\right)
_{j_{1}k_{2}}.
\end{align}


\begin{enumerate}
\item The columns of $U$ (left-singular vectors of $M$) are eigenvectors of
$M^{\dagger}M$ i.e.%
\begin{equation}
M^{\dagger}Mu_{j}=\sigma_{j}^{2}u_{j}%
\end{equation}


\item The \emph{columns} of $V$ (right-singular vectors of $M$) are
eigenvectors of $MM^{\dagger}$ i.e.%
\begin{equation}
MM^{\dagger}z_{j}=\sigma_{j}^{2}z_{j}.
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}

\item One can express $M$ in terms of the general expansions
\begin{align}
M  &  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j,\leq n\\1\leq k\leq m}}E_{z_{j}w_{k}^{d}%
}\mathcal{R}_{zw}\left(  M\right)  _{jk}=\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq
n\\1\leq k_{1}\leq n}}E_{w_{j_{1}}w_{k_{1}}^{d}}\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(
U\right)  _{j_{1}k_{1}}\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{2}\leq n\\1\leq k_{2}\leq
m}}E_{w_{j_{2}}z_{k_{2}}^{d}}\mathcal{R}_{wz}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)
_{j_{2}k_{2}}\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{3}\leq m\\1\leq k_{3}\leq m}%
}E_{z_{j_{3}}z_{k_{3}}^{d}}\mathcal{R}_{z}\left(  Z^{\dagger}\right)
_{j_{3}k_{3}}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1}\leq n}}E_{w_{j_{1}}%
w_{k_{1}}^{d}}\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{j_{1}k_{1}}\sum_{1\leq
j_{2}\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }E_{w_{j_{2}}z_{j_{2}}^{d}}\mathcal{R}%
_{wz}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)  _{j_{2}j_{2}}\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{3}\leq
m\\1\leq k_{3}\leq m}}E_{z_{j_{3}}z_{k_{3}}^{d}}\mathcal{R}_{z}\left(
Z^{\dagger}\right)  _{j_{3}k_{3}}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1}\leq n}}\sum_{1\leq
j_{2}\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{3}\leq m\\1\leq
k_{3}\leq m}}E_{w_{j_{1}}w_{k_{1}}^{d}}E_{w_{j_{2}}z_{j_{2}}^{d}}E_{z_{j_{3}%
}z_{k_{3}}^{d}}\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{j_{1}k_{1}}\mathcal{R}%
_{wz}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)  _{j_{2}j_{2}}\mathcal{R}_{z}\left(  Z^{\dagger
}\right)  _{j_{3}k_{3}}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1}\leq n}}\sum_{1\leq
j_{2}\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{3}\leq m\\1\leq
k_{3}\leq m}}E_{w_{j_{1}}z_{k_{3}}^{d}}\delta_{k_{1}j_{2}}\delta_{j_{2}j_{3}%
}\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{j_{1}k_{1}}\mathcal{R}_{wz}\left(  \U{3a3}
\right)  _{j_{2}j_{2}}\mathcal{R}_{z}\left(  Z^{\dagger}\right)  _{j_{3}k_{3}%
}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{1}\leq n}}\sum_{1\leq
j_{2}\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{3}\leq m\\1\leq
k_{3}\leq m}}E_{w_{j_{1}}z_{k_{3}}^{d}}\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)
_{j_{1}j_{2}}\mathcal{R}_{wz}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)  _{j_{2}j_{2}}%
\mathcal{R}_{z}\left(  Z^{\dagger}\right)  _{j_{2}k_{3}}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{3}\leq m}}E_{w_{j_{1}}%
z_{k_{3}}^{d}}\sum_{1\leq j_{2}\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\mathcal{R}%
_{w}\left(  U\right)  _{j_{1}j_{2}}\mathcal{R}_{wz}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)
_{j_{2}j_{2}}\mathcal{R}_{z}\left(  Z^{\dagger}\right)  _{j_{2}k_{3}}\\
&  =\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq n\\1\leq k_{3}\leq m}}E_{w_{j_{1}}%
z_{k_{3}}^{d}}\mathcal{R}_{wz}\left(  U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger}\right)
_{j_{1}k_{3}}%
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
\sum_{1\leq j_{2}\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sum_{\substack{1\leq j_{1}\leq
n\\1\leq k_{3}\leq m}}E_{w_{j_{1}}v_{k_{3}}^{d}}\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(
U\right)  _{j_{1}j_{2}}\mathcal{R}_{wv}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)  _{j_{2}j_{2}%
}\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  Z^{\dagger}\right)  _{j_{2}k_{3}}%
\end{equation}

\end{enumerate}
\end{remark}

As%
\begin{align}
M^{\dagger}M  &  =(U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger})^{\dagger}(U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger
})=(Z\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}U^{\dagger})(U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger})=Z(\U{3a3} ^{\dagger
}\U{3a3} )Z^{\dagger}\\
MM^{\dagger}  &  =(U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger})(U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger})^{\dagger
}=(U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger})(Z\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}U^{\dagger})=U(\U{3a3} \U{3a3}
^{\dagger})U^{\dagger},
\end{align}
$\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  $ diagonalizes the matrix $\mathcal{R}%
_{v}\left(  M^{\dagger}M\right)  $, while $\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  $
diagonalizes the matrix $\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  MM^{\dagger}\right)  .$

\begin{remark}
The diagonal entries $\U{3c3} _{i}=\mathcal{R}_{wv}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)
_{ii}$ are uniquely determined by $M$ and are known as the singular values of
$M$. The number of non-zero singular values is equal to the rank of $M$. The
columns of $\mathcal{R}_{w}\left(  U\right)  $ and the columns of
$\mathcal{R}_{v}\left(  Z\right)  $ are called \emph{left-singular vectors}
and \emph{right-singular vectors} of $M$, respectively. They form two sets of
orthonormal bases $\left\{  u_{1},...,u_{m}\right\}  $ and $\left\{
z_{1},...,z_{n}\right\}  ,$ and the singular value decomposition of $M$ can be
written as%
\begin{equation}
M=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r\leq\min\left(  m,n\right)  }\sigma_{j}\left[  u_{j}%
z_{j}^{d}\right]  ,
\end{equation}
$r$ is the rank of $M$.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The SVD is not unique, however it is always possible to choose the
decomposition so that the singular values $\U{3c3} _{i}=\mathcal{R}_{\U{3a3}
}\left(  \U{3a3} \right)  _{ii}$ are in descending order. In this case,
$\U{3a3} $ (but not $U$ and $V$) is uniquely determined by $M\in
\mathcal{L}\left(  \mathcal{V},\mathcal{W}\right)  $.
\end{remark}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Range of $M$%
\begin{align}
\operatorname*{Range}\left(  M\right)   &  =\mathbb{C}\text{-}%
\operatorname*{linspan}\left\{  \left.  w_{j}\in\mathcal{W}\right\vert 1\leq
j\leq n\right\} \\
\mathcal{W}  &  =\operatorname*{Range}\left(  M\right)  \oplus
\operatorname*{Range}\left(  M\right)  ^{c}\text{ hence }\operatorname*{Range}%
\left(  M\right)  ^{c}=\left.  \overset{}{\mathcal{W}}\right/
\operatorname*{Range}\left(  M\right) \\
\operatorname*{Range}\left(  M\right)  ^{\bot}  &  =\mathbb{C}\text{-}%
\operatorname*{linspan}\left\{  \left.  w^{d}\left(  w\right)  =0\right\vert
w^{d}\in\mathcal{W}^{d},\forall w\in\operatorname*{Range}\left(  M\right)
\subseteq\mathcal{W}\right\}  .
\end{align}


\item Kernal of $M$%
\begin{align}
\operatorname{Ker}\left(  M\right)   &  =\mathbb{C}\text{-}%
\operatorname*{linspan}\left\{  \left.  Mv=0\right\vert v\in\mathcal{V}%
\right\} \\
\mathcal{V}  &  =\operatorname{Ker}\left(  M\right)  \oplus\operatorname{Ker}%
\left(  M\right)  ^{c}\text{ hence }\operatorname{Ker}\left(  M\right)
^{c}=\left.  \overset{}{\mathcal{V}}\right/  \operatorname{Ker}\left(
M\right) \\
\operatorname{Ker}\left(  M\right)  ^{\bot}  &  =\mathbb{C}\text{-}%
\operatorname*{linspan}\left\{  \left.  v^{d}\left(  v\right)  =0\right\vert
v^{d}\in\mathcal{V}^{d},\forall v\in\operatorname{Ker}\left(  M\right)
\subseteq\mathcal{V}\right\}  .
\end{align}


\item The restriction of $M$
\begin{equation}
M:\operatorname{Ker}\left(  M\right)  ^{c}\rightarrow\operatorname*{Range}%
\left(  M\right)  \subseteq\mathcal{W}.
\end{equation}
is an injection.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Recap of SVD and spectral decomposition}

Singular values, singular vectors, and their relation to the SVD.

\begin{enumerate}
\item A \emph{non-negative} real number $\U{3c3} $ is a singular value for $M$
if and only if there exist unit-length vectors $u$ in $\mathbb{C}^{m}$ and $v$
in $\mathbb{C}^{n}$ such that%
\begin{equation}
Mz=\U{3c3} u
\end{equation}
and $M^{\dagger}u=\U{3c3} v.$

\item The vectors $u$ and $z$ are called \emph{left-singular} and
\emph{right-singular} vectors for $\U{3c3} $, respectively. In any singular
value decomposition%
\begin{equation}
M=U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger}%
\end{equation}
the diagonal entries of $\U{3a3} $ are equal to the singular values of $M$.
The first $p=\min(m,n)$ \underline{\emph{columns}} of $U$ and $Z$ are,
respectively, \emph{left- and right-singular vectors for the corresponding
singular values}. Consequently, the above implies that:

\item An $m\times\ n$ matrix $M$ has at most $p$ distinct singular values.

\begin{enumerate}
\item It is always possible to find a unitary basis $U$ for $\mathcal{W}$ with
a subset of basis vectors spanning the left-singular vectors of each singular
value of $M$.

\item It is always possible to find a unitary basis $Z$ for \mathcal{Z} with a
subset of basis vectors spanning the right-singular vectors of each singular
value of $M$.
\end{enumerate}

\item A singular value for which we can find two left (or right) singular
vectors that are linearly independent is called degenerate. If $u_{1}$ and
$u_{2}$ are two left-singular vectors which both correspond to the singular
value $\U{3c3} $, then any normalized linear combination of the two vectors is
also a left-singular vector corresponding to the singular value $\U{3c3} $.
The similar statement is true for right-singular vectors. The number of
independent left and right-singular vectors coincides, and these singular
vectors appear in the same columns of $U$ and $Z$ corresponding to diagonal
elements of $\U{3a3} $ all with the same value $\U{3c3} $.

\item As an exception, the left and right-singular vectors of singular value
$0$ comprise all unit vectors in the kernel and cokernel, respectively, of
$M$, which by the rank--nullity theorem cannot be the same dimension if $m\neq
n$. Even if all singular values are nonzero, if $m>n$ then the cokernel is
nontrivial, in which case $U$ is padded with $m-n$ orthogonal vectors from the
cokernel. Conversely, if $m<n$, then $Z$ is padded by $n-m$ orthogonal vectors
from the kernel. However, if the singular value of $0$ exists, the extra
columns of $U$ or $Z$ already appear as left or right-singular vectors.

\item Non-degenerate singular values always have unique left- and
right-singular vectors, up to multiplication by a unit-phase factor
$e^{i\U{3c6} }$ (for the real case up to a sign). Consequently, if all
singular values of a square matrix $M$ are non-degenerate and non-zero, then
its singular value decomposition is unique, up to multiplication of a column
of $U$ by a unit-phase factor and simultaneous multiplication of the
corresponding column of $Z$ by the same unit-phase factor. In general, the SVD
is unique up to arbitrary unitary transformations applied uniformly to the
column vectors of both $U$ and $Z$ spanning the subspaces of each singular
value, and up to arbitrary unitary transformations on vectors of $U$ and $Z$
spanning the kernel and cokernel, respectively, of $M$.
\end{enumerate}

\subparagraph{Relation to eigenvalue decomposition}

The singular value decomposition is very general in the sense that it can be
applied to any $m\times\ n$ matrix, whereas eigenvalue decomposition can only
be applied to diagonalizable matrices. Nevertheless, the two decompositions
are related.

Given an SVD of $M$, as described above, the following two relations hold:

\begin{enumerate}
\item
\begin{equation}
M^{\dagger}M=Z\U{3a3} ^{\dagger}U^{\dagger}U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger}=Z(\U{3a3}
^{\dagger}\U{3a3} )Z^{\dagger}MM^{\dagger}=U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger}Z\U{3a3}
^{\dagger}U^{\dagger}=U(\U{3a3} \U{3a3} ^{\dagger})U^{\dagger}%
\end{equation}


\item The right-hand sides of these relations describe the eigenvalue
decompositions of the left-hand sides. Consequently:

\begin{enumerate}
\item The columns of $Z$ (right-singular vectors) are eigenvectors of
$M^{\dagger}M$.

\item The columns of $U$ (left-singular vectors) are eigenvectors of
$MM^{\dagger}$.
\end{enumerate}

\item The non-zero elements of $\U{3a3} $ (non-zero singular values) are the
square roots of the non-zero eigenvalues of $M^{\dagger}M$ or $MM^{\dagger}$.

\item In the special case that $M$ is a normal matrix, which by definition
must be square, the spectral theorem says that it can be unitarily
diagonalized using a basis of eigenvectors, so that it can be written
\begin{equation}
M=UDU^{\dagger}%
\end{equation}
for a unitary matrix $U$ and a diagonal matrix $D$ with complex elements
$\U{3c3} _{i}$ along the diagonal. When $M$ is positive semi-definite,
$\U{3c3} _{i}$ will be non-negative real numbers so that the decomposition is
also a singular value decomposition. Otherwise, it can be recast as an SVD by
moving the phase $e^{i\varphi}$ of each $\U{3c3} _{i}$ to either its
corresponding $Z_{i}$ or $U_{i}.$

\item The natural connection of the SVD to non-normal matrices is through the
polar decomposition

\begin{theorem}
$M=SR$, where $S=U\U{3a3} U^{\dagger}$ is positive semidefinite and normal,
and $R=UV^{\dagger}$ is unitary.
\end{theorem}

\item Thus, except for positive semi-definite matrices, the eigenvalue
decomposition and SVD of $M$, while related, differ: the eigenvalue
decomposition is
\begin{equation}
M=UDU^{-1},
\end{equation}
where $U$ is not necessarily unitary and $D$ is not necessarily positive
semi-definite, while the SVD is
\begin{equation}
M=U\U{3a3} V^{\dagger},
\end{equation}
where $\U{3a3} $ is diagonal and positive semi-definite, and $U$ and $V$ are
unitary matrices that are not necessarily related except through the matrix
$M$.

\begin{remark}
Only non-defective square matrices have an eigenvalue decomposition, any
$m\times\ n$ matrix has a SVD.
\end{remark}
\end{enumerate}

\section{Applications of the SVD}

\subsection{Pseudoinverse}

The singular value decomposition can be used for computing the
\emph{pseudoinverse} of a matrix. (Various authors use different notation for
the pseudoinverse; here we use $\#$. Indeed, the pseudoinverse of the matrix
$M$ with singular value decomposition $M=U\U{3a3} V^{\dagger}$ is%

\begin{equation}
M^{\#}\ =V\U{3a3} ^{\#}U^{\dag}\
\end{equation}
where $\U{3a3} ^{\#}$\ is the pseudoinverse of $\U{3a3} $, which is formed by
replacing every non-zero diagonal entry by its reciprocal and \emph{taking the
adjoint of } the resulting matrix.

\subsection{Solving homogeneous linear equations}

A set of homogeneous linear equations can be written as
\begin{equation}
Ax=0
\end{equation}
for a matrix $A$ and vector $x$. A typical situation is that $A$ is known and
a non-zero $x$ is to be determined which satisfies the equation. Such an $x$
belongs to $A$'s null space and is sometimes called a (right) null vector of
$A$. The vector $x$ can be characterized as a right-singular vector
corresponding to a singular value of $A$ that is zero. This observation means
that if $A$ is a square matrix and has no vanishing singular value, the
equation has no non-zero $x$ as a solution. It also means that if there are
several vanishing singular values, any linear combination of the corresponding
right-singular vectors is a valid solution. Analogously to the definition of a
(right) null vector, a non-zero $x$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
x^{\dagger}A=0,
\end{equation}
with $x^{\dagger}$ denoting the conjugate transpose of $x$, is called a left
null vector of $A$.

\subsection{Total least squares minimization}

A total least squares problem seeks the vector $x$ that minimizes the $2$-norm
of a vector $Ax$ under the constraint $||x||\,=1$. The solution turns out to
be the right-singular vector of $A$ corresponding to the smallest singular value.

\subsection{Range, Null Space and Rank}

Another application of the SVD is that it provides an explicit representation
of the range and null space of a matrix $M$. The right-singular vectors
corresponding to vanishing singular vectors of $M$ span the null space of $M$
and the left-singular vectors corresponding to the non-zero singular values of
$M$ span the range of $M$.

As a consequence, the rank of $M$ equals the number of non-zero singular
values which is the same as the number of non-zero diagonal elements in
$\U{3a3} $ . In numerical linear algebra the singular values can be used to
determine the effective rank of a matrix, as rounding error may lead to small
but non-zero singular values in a rank deficient matrix. Singular values
beyond a significant gap are assumed to be numerically equivalent to zero.

\subsection{Low-rank matrix approximation}

Some practical applications need to solve the problem of approximating a
matrix $M$ with another matrix $\widetilde{M}$, said to be truncated, which
has a specific rank $r$. In the case that the approximation is based on
minimizing the Frobenius norm of the difference between $M$ and $\widetilde{M}%
$ under the constraint that $\operatorname*{Rank}(\widetilde{M})=r$, it turns
out that the solution is given by the SVD of $M$, namely%

\begin{equation}
\widetilde{M}=U\widetilde{\U{3a3} }Z^{\dagger}%
\end{equation}
where $\widetilde{\U{3a3} }$ is the same matrix as $\U{3a3} $ except that it
contains only the $r$ largest singular values (the other singular values are
replaced by zero). This is known as the Eckart--Young theorem, as it was
proved by those two authors in 1936 (although it was later found to have been
known to earlier authors; see Stewart 1993).

\subsection{Separable models}

The SVD can be thought of as decomposing a matrix into a weighted, ordered sum
of separable matrices. By separable, we mean that a matrix $A$ can be written
as an tensor product of two vectors $A=u\otimes v$, or, in coordinates,
$A_{ij}=u_{i}v_{j}$. Specifically, the matrix $M$ can be decomposed as%

\begin{equation}
M=\sum_{i}A_{i}=\sum_{i}\U{3c3} _{i}U_{i}\otimes Z_{i}.
\end{equation}
Here $U_{i}$ and $Z_{i}$ are the $i$-th columns of the corresponding SVD
matrices, $\U{3c3} _{i}$ are the ordered singular values, and each $A_{i}$ is separable.

\subsection{Nearest orthogonal matrix}

It is possible to use the SVD of a square matrix $A$ to determine the
orthogonal matrix $O$ closest to $A$. The closeness of fit is measured by the
Frobenius norm of $O-A.$ The solution is the product $UZ^{\dagger}$. This
intuitively makes sense because an orthogonal matrix would have the
decomposition $UIZ^{\dagger}$ where $I$ is the identity matrix, so that if
$A=U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger}$ then the product $A=UZ^{\dagger}$ amounts to
replacing the singular values with ones. Equivalently, the solution is the
unitary matrix $R=UZ^{\dagger}$ of the Polar Decomposition $M=RP=P\prime R$ in
either order of stretch and rotation, as described above.

\subsection{Signal processing}

The SVD and pseudoinverse have been successfully applied to signal processing,
image processing and big data (e.g., in genomic signal processing).

\subsection{Existence proofs}

An eigenvalue $\U{3bb} $ of a matrix $M$ is characterized by the algebraic
relation $Mu=\U{3bb} u$. When $M$ is Hermitian, a variational characterization
is also available. Let $M$ be a real $n\times\ n$ symmetric matrix. Define%
\begin{align}
f  &  :\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}\\
f  &  :x\mapsto x^{\dagger}Mx
\end{align}
By the extreme value theorem, this continuous function attains a maximum at
some $u$ when restricted to the unit sphere $||x||\,=1$. By the Lagrange
multipliers theorem, $u$ necessarily satisfies%
\begin{equation}
\left(  \nabla u\right)  ^{\dagger}Mu-\U{3bb} \left(  \nabla u\right)
^{\dagger}u=0
\end{equation}
for some real number $\U{3bb} $. The nabla symbol, $\nabla$, is the del
operator (differentiation with respect to $x$). Using the symmetry of $M$ we obtain%

\begin{equation}
\left(  \nabla x\right)  ^{\dagger}Mx-\U{3bb} \left(  \nabla x\right)
^{\dagger}x=2(M-\U{3bb} I)x.
\end{equation}


Therefore
\begin{equation}
Mu=\U{3bb} u,
\end{equation}
so $u$ is a unit length eigenvector of $M$. For every unit length eigenvector
$v$ of $M$ its eigenvalue is $f(v)$, so $\U{3bb} $ is the largest eigenvalue
of $M$. The same calculation performed on the orthogonal complement of $u$
gives the next largest eigenvalue and so on. The complex Hermitian case is
similar; there
\begin{equation}
f(x)=x^{\dagger}Mx
\end{equation}
is a real-valued function of $2n$ real variables.

Singular values are similar in that they can be described algebraically or
from variational principles. Although, unlike the eigenvalue case,
Hermiticity, or symmetry, of $M$ is no longer required.

\subsubsection{Based on the spectral theorem}

Let $M$ be an $m\times\ n$ complex matrix. Since $M^{\dagger}M$ is positive
semi-definite and Hermitian, by the spectral theorem, there exists an $n\times
n$ unitary matrix $Z$ such that%

\begin{equation}
Z^{\dagger}M^{\dagger}MZ=D=\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\overline{D} & 0\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  ,
\end{equation}
where $\overline{D}$ is diagonal and positive definite, of dimension
$\ell\times\ \ell$ , with $\ell\leq\min(n,m)$ the number of non-zero
eigenvalues of $M^{\dagger}M$ . Note that $Z$ is here by definition a matrix
whose $i$-th column is the $i$-th eigenvector of $M^{\dagger}M$ ,
corresponding to the eigenvalue $\overline{D}_{ii}$. Moreover, the $j$-th
column of $Z$ , for $j>\ell$ , is an eigenvector of $M^{\dagger}M$ with
eigenvalue $D_{jj}=0$ . This can be expressed by writing $Z$ as $Z=[Z_{1}%
Z_{2}]$, where the columns of $Z_{1}$ and $Z_{2}$ therefore contain the
eigenvectors of $M^{\dagger}M$ corresponding to non-zero and zero eigenvalues,
respectively. Using this rewriting of $Z$, the equation becomes:%
\begin{equation}
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
Z_{1}^{\dagger}\\
Z_{2}^{\dagger}%
\end{array}
\right)  M^{\dagger}M\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
Z_{1} & Z_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
Z_{1}^{\dagger}M^{\dagger}MZ_{1} & Z_{1}^{\dagger}M^{\dagger}MZ_{2}\\
Z_{2}^{\dagger}M^{\dagger}MZ_{1} & Z_{2}^{\dagger}M^{\dagger}MZ_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
\overline{D} & 0\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}


This implies that%
\begin{align}
Z_{1}^{\dagger}M^{\dagger}MZ_{1}  &  =\overline{D}\\
Z_{2}^{\dagger}M^{\dagger}MZ_{1}  &  =0\\
Z_{2}^{\dagger}M^{\dagger}MZ_{2}  &  =0.
\end{align}


Moreover, the second equation implies $MZ_{2}=0$ Finally, the unitary-ness of
$Z_{2}$ gives the following conditions:%
\begin{align}
Z_{1}^{\dagger}Z_{1}  &  =I_{1},\\
Z_{2}^{\dagger}Z_{2}  &  =I_{2},\\
Z_{1}Z_{1}^{\dagger}+Z_{2}Z_{2}^{\dagger}  &  =I_{12},
\end{align}


where the subscripts on the identity matrices are used to denote that they are
of different dimensions.

Let us now define%
\begin{equation}
U_{1}=MZ_{1}D^{-\frac{1}{2}}.
\end{equation}
Then,%
\begin{equation}
U_{1}D^{\frac{1}{2}}Z_{1}^{\dagger}=MZ_{1}D^{-\frac{1}{2}}D^{\frac{1}{2}}%
Z_{1}^{\dagger}=M(I-Z_{2}Z_{2}^{\dagger})=M-(MZ_{2})Z_{2}^{\dagger}=M,
\end{equation}
since $MZ_{2}=0$. This can be also seen as immediate consequence of the fact
that $MZ_{1}Z_{1}^{\dagger}=M$ . This is equivalent to the observation that if
$\{\left.  v_{i}\right\vert 1\leq i\leq l\}$ is the set of eigenvectors of
$M^{\dagger}M$ corresponding to non-vanishing eigenvalues $\{\left.  \U{3bb}
_{i}\right\vert 1\leq i\leq l\}$, then $\{\left.  Mv_{i}\right\vert 1\leq
i\leq l\}$ is a set of orthogonal vectors, and $\{\left.  \U{3bb} _{i}%
^{-\frac{1}{2}}Mv_{i}\right\vert 1\leq i\leq l\}$ is a (generally not
complete) set of orthonormal vectors. This matches with the matrix formalism
used above denoting with $Z_{1}$ the matrix whose columns are $\{\left.
v_{i}\right\vert 1\leq i\leq l\}$, with $Z_{2}$ the matrix whose columns are
the eigenvectors of $M^{\dagger}M$ with vanishing eigenvalue, and $U_{1}$ the
matrix whose columns are the vectors $\{\left.  \U{3bb} _{i}^{-\frac{1}{2}%
}Mv_{i}\right\vert 1\leq i\leq l\}$.

We see that this is almost the desired result, except that $U_{1}$ and $U_{1}$
are in general not unitary, since they might not be square. However, we do
know that the number of rows of $U_{1}$ is no smaller than the number of
columns, since the dimensions of $D$ is no greater than $m$ and $n$ . Also,
since%
\begin{equation}
U_{1}^{\dagger}U_{1}=D^{-\frac{1}{2}}Z_{1}^{\dagger}M^{\dagger}MZ_{1}%
D^{-\frac{1}{2}}=D^{-\frac{1}{2}}DD^{-\frac{1}{2}}=I_{1},
\end{equation}
the columns in $U_{1}$ are orthonormal and can be extended to an orthonormal
basis. This means that we can choose $U_{2}$ such that $U=[U_{1}U_{2}]$ is unitary.

For $Z_{1}$ we already have $Z_{2}$ to make it unitary. Now, define%
\begin{equation}
\U{3a3} =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D^{\frac{1}{2}} & 0\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right) \\%
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
0 & 0
\end{array}
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}
where extra zero rows are added or removed to make the number of zero rows
equal the number of columns of $U_{2}$, and hence the overall dimensions of
$\U{3a3} $ equal to $m\times\ n$ . Then%
\begin{equation}
\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
U_{1} & U_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
D^{\frac{1}{2}} & 0\\
0 & 0
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
Z_{1}^{\dagger}\\
Z_{2}^{\dagger}%
\end{array}
\right)  =\left(
\begin{array}
[c]{cc}%
U_{1} & U_{2}%
\end{array}
\right)  \left(
\begin{array}
[c]{c}%
D^{\frac{1}{2}}Z_{1}^{\dagger}\\
0
\end{array}
\right)  =U_{1}D^{\frac{1}{2}}Z_{1}^{\dagger}=M,
\end{equation}
which is the desired result:%
\begin{equation}
M=U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}


Notice the argument could begin with diagonalizing $MM^{\dagger}$ rather than
$M^{\dagger}M$ (This shows directly that $MM^{\dagger}$ and $M^{\dagger}M$
have the same non-zero eigenvalues).

\subsubsection{Based on variational characterization}

The singular values can also be characterized as the maxima of $u^{\dagger}%
Mv$, considered as a function of $u$ and $v$, over particular subspaces. The
singular vectors are the values of $u$ and $v$ where these maxima are attained.

Let $M$ denote an $m\times\ n$ matrix with complex entries. Let $S^{k-1}$ be
the unit $(k-1)$-sphere in $\mathbb{C}^{k}$, and define
\begin{equation}
f(u,v)=u^{\dagger}Mv,\quad u\in S^{m-1},v\in S^{n-1}.
\end{equation}


Consider the function $f$ restricted to $S^{m-1}\times\ S^{n-1}$. Since both
$S^{m-1}$ and $S^{n-1}$ are compact sets, their product is also compact.
Furthermore, since $f$ is continuous, it attains a largest value for at least
one pair of vectors $u\in S^{m-1}$ and $v\in S^{n-1}$. This largest value is
denoted $\U{3c3} _{1}$ and the corresponding vectors are denoted $u_{1}$ and
$v_{1}$. Since $\U{3c3} _{1}$ is the largest value of $f(u,v)$ it must be
non-negative. If it were negative, changing the sign of either $u_{1}$ or
$v_{1}$ would make it positive and therefore larger.

\begin{theorem}
$u_{1},v_{1}$ are left and right-singular vectors of $M$ with corresponding
singular value $\U{3c3} _{1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Similar to the eigenvalues case, by assumption the two vectors satisfy the
Lagrange multiplier equation:%
\begin{equation}
\nabla\U{3c3} =\left(  \nabla u\right)  ^{\dagger}Mv-\U{3bb} _{1}\left(
\nabla u\right)  ^{\dagger}u-\U{3bb} _{2}\left(  \nabla v\right)  ^{\dagger}v
\end{equation}
After some algebra, this becomes%
\begin{equation}
Mv_{1}=2\U{3bb} _{1}u_{1}+0M^{\dagger}u_{1}=0+2\U{3bb} _{2}v_{1}=2\U{3bb}
_{2}v_{1}%
\end{equation}
Multiplying the first equation from left by $u_{1}^{\dagger}$ and the second
equation from left by $v_{1}^{\dagger}$ and taking
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert u\right\Vert =\left\Vert v\right\Vert =1
\end{equation}
into account gives%
\begin{equation}
\U{3c3} _{1}=2\U{3bb} _{1}=2\U{3bb} _{2}.
\end{equation}
Plugging this into the pair of equations above, we have%
\begin{equation}
Mv_{1}=\U{3c3} _{1}u_{1}M^{\dagger}u_{1}=\U{3c3} _{1}v_{1}%
\end{equation}

\end{proof}

$\blacksquare$

More singular vectors and singular values can be found by maximizing $f(u,v)$
over normalized $u,v$ which are orthogonal to $u_{1}$ and $v_{1}$, respectively.

\subsection{Norms}

\subsubsection{Ky Fan norms}

\begin{enumerate}
\item The sum of the $k$ largest singular values of $M$ is a matrix norm, the
Ky Fan $k$-norm of $M$.

\item The first of the Ky Fan norms, the Ky Fan $1$-norm, is the same as the
operator norm of $M$ as a linear operator with respect to the Euclidean norms
of $K^{m}$ and $K^{n}$. In other words, the Ky Fan $1$-norm is the operator
norm induced by the standard $\ell_{2}$ Euclidean inner product. For this
reason, it is also called the operator $2$-norm.

\item One can easily verify the relationship between the Ky Fan $1$-norm and
singular values. It is true in general, for a bounded operator $M$ on
(possibly infinite-dimensional) Hilbert spaces$\Vert M\Vert=||M^{\dagger
}M||^{\frac{1}{2}}$

\item But, in the matrix case, $\left(  M^{\dagger}M\right)  ^{^{\frac{1}{2}}%
}$ is a normal matrix, so $||M^{\dagger}M||^{\frac{1}{2}}$ is the largest
eigenvalue of $(M\ast M)^{\frac{1}{2}}$, i.e. the largest singular value of
$M$.

\item The last of the Ky Fan norms, the sum of all singular values, is the
trace norm (also known as the 'nuclear norm'), defined by $\Vert
M\Vert=\left\langle \left.  M\right\vert M\right\rangle ^{\frac{1}{2}}$ (the
eigenvalues of $M^{\dagger}M$ are the squares of the singular values).
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Hilbert--Schmidt norm}

The singular values are related to another norm on the space of operators.
Consider the Hilbert--Schmidt inner product on the $n\times\ n$ matrices,
defined by%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left.  N\right\vert M\right\rangle =\operatorname{Tr}\U{2061}
(N^{\dagger}M),
\end{equation}
so the induced norm is%
\begin{equation}
\Vert M\Vert\,=\left\langle \left.  M\right\vert M\right\rangle ^{\frac{1}{2}%
}=\operatorname{Tr}\U{2061} (M^{\dagger}M)
\end{equation}
Since the trace is invariant under unitary equivalence, this shows%
\begin{equation}
\Vert M\Vert\,=\sum_{i}\U{3c3} _{i}^{2},
\end{equation}
where $\U{3c3} _{i}$ are the singular values of $M$. This is called the
Frobenius norm, Schatten $2$-norm, or Hilbert--Schmidt norm of $M$. Direct
calculation shows that the Frobenius norm of $M=\left\{  m_{ij}\right\}  $
coincides with: $\U{2211} _{ij}|m_{ij}|^{2}$.

\begin{remark}
In addition, the Frobenius norm and the trace norm (the nuclear norm) are
special cases of the Schatten norm.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Tensor SVD}

Two types of tensor decompositions exist, which generalise the SVD to multi arrays.

\begin{enumerate}
\item One of them decomposes a tensor into a sum of rank-$1$ tensors, which is
called a \emph{tensor rank decomposition}.

\item The second type of decomposition computes the orthonormal subspaces
associated with the different factors appearing in the tensor product of
vector spaces in which the tensor lives. This decomposition is referred to in
the literature as the higher-order \emph{SVD (HOSVD)} or
$\emph{Tucker3/TuckerM}$.

\item In addition, multilinear principal component analysis in multilinear
subspace learning involves the same mathematical operations as Tucker
decomposition, being used in a different context of dimensionality reduction.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Scale-invariant SVD}

The singular values of a matrix $A$ are uniquely defined and are invariant
with respect to left and/or right unitary transformations of $A$. In other
words, the singular values of $UAZ$, for unitary $U$ and $Z$, are equal to the
singular values of $A$. This is an important property for applications in
which it is necessary to preserve Euclidean distances and invariance with
respect to rotations.

The Scale-Invariant SVD, or SI-SVD, is analogous to the conventional SVD
except that its uniquely-determined singular values are invariant with respect
to diagonal transformations of $A$. In other words, the singular values of
$DAE$, for invertible diagonal matrices $D$ and $E$, are equal to the singular
values of $A$. This is an important property for applications for which
invariance to the choice of units on variables (e.g., metric versus imperial
units) is needed.

\subsection{Bounded operators on Hilbert spaces}

The factorization $M=U\U{3a3} Z^{\dagger}$ can be extended to a bounded
operator $M$ on a separable Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$. Namely, for any
bounded operator $M$, there exist a partial isometry $U$, a unitary $Z$, a
measure space $(X,%
%TCIMACRO{\U{3bc} }%
%BeginExpansion
\mu
%EndExpansion
)$, and a non-negative measurable $f$ such that $M=UT_{f}Z^{\dagger}$ where
$T_{f}$ is the multiplication by $f$ on $L_{2}(X,%
%TCIMACRO{\U{3bc} }%
%BeginExpansion
\mu
%EndExpansion
)$.

This can be shown by mimicking the linear algebraic argument for the matricial
case above. $ZT_{f}Z^{\dagger}$ is the unique positive square root of
$M^{\dagger}M$, as given by the Borel functional calculus for self-adjoint
operators. The reason why $U$ need not be unitary is because, unlike the
finite-dimensional case, given an isometry $U_{1}$ with nontrivial kernel, a
suitable $U_{2}$ may not be found such that $[U_{1}U_{2}]$ is a unitary operator.

As for matrices, the singular value factorization is equivalent to the polar
decomposition for operators: we can simply write%

\begin{equation}
M=\left(  UZ^{\dagger}\right)  \left(  ZT_{f}Z^{\dagger}\right)
\end{equation}
and notice that $UZ^{\dagger}$ is still a partial isometry while
$ZT_{f}Z^{\dagger}$ is positive.

\subsubsection{Singular values and compact operators}

The notion of singular values and left/right-singular vectors can be extended
to compact operator on Hilbert space as they have a discrete spectrum. If $T$
is compact, every non-zero $\U{3bb} $ in its spectrum is an eigenvalue.
Furthermore, a compact self-adjoint operator can be diagonalized by its
eigenvectors. If $M$ is compact, so is $M^{\dagger}M$. Applying the
diagonalization result, the unitary image of its positive square root $T_{f}$
has a set of orthonormal eigenvectors $\{e_{i}\}$ corresponding to strictly
positive eigenvalues $\{\U{3c3} _{i}\}$. For any $\U{3c8} \in\mathcal{H}$,%

\begin{equation}
M\U{3c8} =UT_{f}Z^{\dagger}\U{3c8} =\sum_{i}\left\langle \left.
UT_{f}Z^{\dagger}\U{3c8} \right\vert ,Ue_{i}\right\rangle Ue_{i}=\sum
_{i}\U{3c3} _{i}\left\langle \left.  \U{3c8} \right\vert Ze_{i}\right\rangle
Ue_{i},
\end{equation}
where the series converges in the norm topology on $\mathcal{H}$. Notice how
this resembles the expression from the finite-dimensional case. $\U{3c3} _{i}$
are called the singular values of $M$. $\{Ue_{i}\}$ (resp. $\{Ze_{i}\}$) can
be considered the left-singular (resp. right-singular) vectors of $M$.

\begin{remark}
Compact operators on a Hilbert space are the closure of finite-rank operators
in the uniform operator topology. The above series expression gives an
explicit such representation. An immediate consequence of this is:
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}
$M$ is compact if and only if $M^{\dagger}M$ is compact.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{History}

The singular value decomposition was originally developed by differential
geometers, who wished to determine whether a real bilinear form could be made
equal to another by independent orthogonal transformations of the two spaces
it acts on. Eugenio Beltrami and Camille Jordan discovered independently, in
1873 and 1874 respectively, that the singular values of the bilinear forms,
represented as a matrix, form a complete set of invariants for bilinear forms
under orthogonal substitutions. James Joseph Sylvester also arrived at the
singular value decomposition for real square matrices in 1889, apparently
independently of both Beltrami and Jordan. Sylvester called the singular
values the canonical multipliers of the matrix $A$. The fourth mathematician
to discover the singular value decomposition independently is Autonne in 1915,
who arrived at it via the polar decomposition. The first proof of the singular
value decomposition for rectangular and complex matrices seems to be by Carl
Eckart and Gale J. Young in 1936 they saw it as a generalization of the
principal axis transformation for Hermitian matrices.

In 1907, Erhard Schmidt defined an analog of singular values for integral
operators (which are compact, under some weak technical assumptions); it seems
he was unaware of the parallel work on singular values of finite matrices.
This theory was further developed by \'{E}mile Picard in 1910, who is the
first to call the numbers $\U{3c3} _{k}$ singular values (or in French,
valeurs singuli\`{e}res).

Practical methods for computing the SVD date back to Kogbetliantz in
1954--1955 and Hestenes in 1958 resembling closely the Jacobi eigenvalue
algorithm, which uses plane rotations or Givens rotations. However, these were
replaced by the method of Gene Golub and William Kahan published in 1965,
which uses Householder transformations or reflections. In 1970, Golub and
Christian Reinsch published a variant of the Golub/Kahan algorithm that is
still the one most-used today.
\end{document}